  {
    "topicTitle": "Assertions",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-1",
        "title": "Assertions — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals",
        "content": "Assertions are executable checks that compare the actual result of a test with the behavior the application is expected to produce. In Java testing, assertions turn requirements into verifiable conditions: a returned value must match an expected value, an object must not be null, a collection must contain the required element, or an operation must fail in a specified way. JUnit Jupiter provides assertion methods through org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions, which can be imported statically to keep test code readable. A useful assertion should express one clear business expectation and should fail with enough context to explain the mismatch. For example, a service test can assert that a successful order operation returns the expected order status and generated identifier. Assertions do not replace good test design: first arrange the input and dependencies, execute the behavior, and then assert the observable outcome. Avoid assertions that merely repeat implementation details, such as checking private internal fields when the public contract is what matters. In production CI pipelines, meaningful assertions help detect regressions early and make failures actionable for developers."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-2",
        "title": "Assertions — Framework Architecture",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter assertions are part of the JUnit 5 programming model and are supplied by the junit-jupiter-api dependency. Common methods include assertEquals, assertNotEquals, assertTrue, assertFalse, assertNull, assertNotNull, assertSame, assertNotSame, assertArrayEquals, and assertIterableEquals. These methods accept an optional failure message, and many also support a message supplier so the message is created only when the assertion fails. Static imports are commonly used, for example import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals. The expected and actual arguments must be placed in the correct order: assertEquals(expected, actual) makes the intended comparison obvious in a failure report. Assertions can also be combined with assertAll to report multiple independent failures from one test. The assertion API should be selected according to the contract being tested rather than choosing a generic boolean check for every condition. For example, assertNotNull communicates more clearly than assertTrue(result != null), while assertThrows communicates exception behavior more precisely than manually catching an exception. Understanding the API helps teams write consistent tests that are easy to review and diagnose."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-3",
        "title": "Assertions — Test Design and Implementation",
        "content": "A reliable assertion strategy begins by identifying the observable contract of the method or component under test. For a calculation method, verify the exact result and relevant boundary values; for a repository, verify the returned records and ordering only when ordering is part of the contract; for a REST service, verify status, response data, and meaningful headers. Separate setup, execution, and verification so that the reason for a failure is easy to identify. Use multiple assertions when they describe related properties of one result, but avoid turning one test into a long checklist for unrelated business scenarios. Parameterized tests can apply the same assertion logic to several input cases, while separate tests should cover distinct behaviors such as valid input, invalid input, and boundary conditions. Prefer deterministic expected values over values calculated using the same algorithm as the production code, because duplicating the implementation can reproduce the same defect in both places. Include boundary cases such as empty collections, zero values, maximum allowed values, missing fields, and unexpected states. This approach makes assertions useful as executable acceptance criteria and reduces false confidence from tests that only verify that code ran without throwing an error."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-4",
        "title": "Assertions — Assertions and Lifecycle",
        "content": "Equality assertions verify that expected and actual values are equal according to their equality contract. Use assertEquals for value comparison, including strings, numbers, enums, and objects whose equals method represents meaningful equality. Use assertNotEquals when a result must differ from a known value. Use assertSame and assertNotSame only when object identity matters, such as verifying that a cached instance is reused or that two references point to different objects. For arrays, assertArrayEquals compares array contents, while assertIterableEquals checks iterable elements in iteration order. Be careful with floating-point calculations: exact equality may be unsuitable when rounding is involved, so use the overload that accepts a delta and choose a tolerance justified by the domain. For BigDecimal, scale can affect equals behavior; decide whether scale is part of the requirement or whether compareTo-based numerical equality is appropriate. Collection assertions should verify size and contents when both are important, without making assumptions about order for unordered data structures. A useful failure message should identify the scenario and expected value. These choices prevent tests from passing or failing for reasons unrelated to the actual application requirement."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-5",
        "title": "Assertions — Mocking and Dependency Isolation",
        "content": "Null and boolean assertions communicate important preconditions and outcomes. Use assertNull when the contract explicitly requires an absent value, and assertNotNull when a result must be present before its fields are inspected. Use assertTrue and assertFalse for predicates, feature flags, authorization decisions, and other boolean outcomes. Avoid expressions that hide the behavior being verified; a direct assertion such as assertTrue(account.isActive()) is clearer than a complicated expression combining several conditions. If a null result is not expected, assertNotNull first and then assert the object's meaningful properties. This produces a clearer failure than allowing a later NullPointerException to occur during test execution. For optional values, verify presence with an appropriate assertion and then inspect the contained value. For collections, distinguish between a null collection and an empty collection because they can represent different API contracts. Boolean assertions should focus on one condition at a time so the test report identifies exactly what failed. In service and API tests, these assertions can validate authentication state, feature availability, validation outcomes, and whether a requested operation was accepted or rejected."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-6",
        "title": "Assertions — Unit and Integration Testing",
        "content": "Exception assertions verify that invalid or exceptional operations fail in the expected way. JUnit Jupiter provides assertThrows, which executes a supplied executable and returns the captured exception when the expected type is thrown. The returned exception can then be inspected for an error code, message, or other contractually important detail. Use assertThrowsExactly when the exact exception class is required; use assertThrows when an instance of the expected type or a subtype is acceptable. Keep the executable passed to assertThrows focused on the operation expected to fail. If unrelated setup or verification code is included in the lambda, an exception from the wrong statement may make the test misleading. Use assertDoesNotThrow when successful execution without an exception is itself an important requirement, but still assert the returned result when the operation produces one. Exception tests should cover meaningful invalid inputs, authorization failures, missing resources, and dependency failures. Avoid checking fragile full exception messages when wording is not part of the public contract; prefer stable error codes or important message fragments. Correct exception assertions protect error-handling behavior and prevent applications from silently accepting invalid operations."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-7",
        "title": "Assertions — Container-Based Testing",
        "content": "Grouped assertions use assertAll to execute several independent assertions and report multiple failures together. This is especially useful when verifying a response object with several independent fields, such as an order identifier, status, currency, and total amount. Without assertAll, the first failed assertion stops the remaining assertions in that test method, which can require repeated CI runs to discover additional mismatches. A grouped assertion can accept an executable or a heading followed by executable assertions, making the failure report easier to understand. Use grouping only when all checks are meaningful for the same scenario and can safely be evaluated even if another check fails. Do not group dependent checks where a later assertion requires an earlier value to be valid; first verify that the required object exists, then inspect its fields. Nested assertAll groups can organize complex result structures, but excessive nesting makes test reports difficult to read. Grouped assertions do not mean that unrelated scenarios should be combined into one test. A clear test name, a descriptive group heading, and independent checks provide useful diagnostics while preserving the readability and maintainability of the test suite."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-8",
        "title": "Assertions — Coverage and Quality Measurement",
        "content": "Timeout assertions protect tests against operations that must complete within a defined time limit. JUnit Jupiter provides assertTimeout and assertTimeoutPreemptively. assertTimeout measures the execution duration and reports a failure if the supplied executable exceeds the configured duration, but the executable is allowed to finish before the assertion returns. assertTimeoutPreemptively runs the executable in a separate thread and can interrupt the execution when the timeout is exceeded. The preemptive form requires caution because code running in another thread may not behave like code on the test thread, especially when it relies on ThreadLocal state, transaction context, security context, or framework-managed resources. A timeout assertion is appropriate for verifying a documented performance boundary or detecting an operation that unexpectedly hangs. It is not a replacement for a proper performance test, load test, or production monitoring system. Avoid extremely small timeout values that are unstable on shared CI agents, where CPU contention and machine variability can cause false failures. Choose realistic limits, keep the measured operation focused, and investigate repeated timeout failures rather than simply increasing the limit without understanding the cause."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-9",
        "title": "Assertions — Test Reporting",
        "content": "Custom assertions improve readability when the same domain-specific verification is repeated across many tests. A team can create a helper method such as assertValidCustomer or assertSuccessfulDeployment that checks a collection of related business conditions and reports failures using meaningful domain language. Custom assertions should be implemented using the standard JUnit assertion methods so that failures integrate correctly with the test framework and reporting tools. Keep helpers small, deterministic, and focused on one domain concept. Avoid hiding substantial setup, network calls, or state changes inside an assertion helper because verification should remain easy to distinguish from execution. For complex objects, assertion libraries can provide fluent methods for checking nested properties, collections, and object graphs; the team should select a library that fits its build and dependency policies. When writing reusable assertion helpers, include clear failure messages and test the helper itself when its logic is non-trivial. A well-designed custom assertion reduces duplication and gives tests a vocabulary that matches the business domain, while a poorly designed helper can obscure what a test actually verifies."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-10",
        "title": "Assertions — Flaky Test Investigation",
        "content": "Assertion failures should provide enough information for a developer to reproduce and correct the defect. Include scenario-specific messages for important checks, especially when the same test logic is executed for several input cases. JUnit supports message suppliers, which defer message construction until an assertion fails and can avoid unnecessary string creation during successful test execution. For parameterized tests, include the input value or case identifier in the failure context so the failed example can be identified immediately. Avoid messages such as \"test failed\" or \"value is wrong\"; describe the expected behavior and the relevant scenario. When an assertion fails in CI, inspect the expected value, actual value, test input, recent code changes, and any external data used by the test. Be alert to time-dependent, random, locale-dependent, or environment-specific values that make expected results unstable. Do not weaken a valid assertion simply to obtain a green pipeline. Correct the implementation, fix an invalid test assumption, or isolate a genuinely external dependency using an appropriate test double. Clear diagnostics reduce investigation time and help teams distinguish application defects from test-environment problems."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-4-section-11",
        "title": "Assertions — CI Integration",
        "content": "Assertions are a critical part of automated quality gates in a CI pipeline. During a Maven or Gradle build, JUnit tests execute and assertion failures cause the test task to report a failure, allowing the pipeline to stop before packaging or deployment according to the configured quality policy. A reliable pipeline should preserve test reports and logs even when a test stage fails, so developers can inspect the exact assertion and test case. Configure the build to fail when tests fail; do not routinely skip tests or ignore failure exit codes to make a pipeline appear successful. Separate fast unit tests from slower integration tests when that improves feedback time, but retain both as required quality gates for the relevant changes. If failures occur only on CI, compare Java versions, environment variables, time zones, locale, database state, dependency versions, and parallel execution settings between local and pipeline environments. Flaky assertions should be investigated for shared mutable state, race conditions, random inputs, and timing assumptions. Track failure trends and keep test reports accessible to the team. Strong assertion practices make CI results trustworthy and prevent defective changes from reaching production environments."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Test Lifecycle",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-1",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — Understanding the JUnit 5 Test Lifecycle",
        "content": "The JUnit 5 test lifecycle describes the sequence of operations performed by the Jupiter test engine when it discovers and executes a test class. Understanding this sequence is essential because test results can depend on when objects are created, when preparation methods execute, and when cleanup occurs. A typical test execution includes test instance creation, extension callbacks, before-all methods, before-each methods, the test method, after-each methods, and after-all methods. The exact sequence depends on the test instance lifecycle mode, nested test structure, extensions, and execution configuration. For example, a service test may create a fresh test object, initialize mock dependencies before each test, execute an assertion, and release temporary resources afterward. Developers should not assume that all test classes share one object or that cleanup happens only when a test succeeds. JUnit manages lifecycle callbacks even when tests fail, subject to process termination and other exceptional conditions. A clear lifecycle design prevents shared-state defects, resource leaks, and order-dependent tests. When investigating an unexpected test result, inspect the lifecycle annotations and execution order before changing application code."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-2",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — PER_METHOD and PER_CLASS Test Instances",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter supports two important test instance lifecycle modes: PER_METHOD and PER_CLASS. PER_METHOD is the default mode. JUnit creates a new test class instance for each test method, which helps isolate instance fields because one test cannot normally change the instance state observed by another test. PER_CLASS creates one test instance for the entire test class, allowing instance fields and setup state to be shared across test methods. A class can select this mode with @TestInstance(TestInstance.Lifecycle.PER_CLASS). One practical reason to use PER_CLASS is to allow a non-static @BeforeAll or @AfterAll method. However, shared instance fields introduce risks: a test that changes a collection, counter, or mock configuration may affect a later test. This can cause failures that appear only when execution order changes or tests run in parallel. For example, a test class that shares an in-memory repository must reset its contents before each test. Choose PER_METHOD when independent test instances provide simple isolation. Choose PER_CLASS only when shared instance state is intentional and safely controlled. Never depend on an assumed test execution order to make shared state correct."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-3",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — @BeforeAll and @AfterAll Execution",
        "content": "@BeforeAll and @AfterAll define class-level lifecycle operations in JUnit Jupiter. A @BeforeAll method runs once before the test methods in its test class begin, while @AfterAll runs once after the class test execution has finished. They are suitable for expensive class-wide initialization and cleanup, such as starting a shared test server or preparing an immutable reference dataset. With the default PER_METHOD lifecycle, these methods normally must be static because no single test instance exists for the whole class. With PER_CLASS, they can be instance methods. A typical example is starting a local HTTP stub in @BeforeAll, storing its endpoint, and stopping it in @AfterAll. The cleanup method should release resources even when individual tests fail. Avoid placing mutable per-test data in class-level setup because it can leak between tests. Also remember that class-level callbacks do not automatically provide isolation between parallel test methods. If the resource is shared, protect it appropriately or disable concurrent access for that resource. Use these annotations for genuinely class-wide resources, not as a replacement for @BeforeEach and @AfterEach."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-4",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — @BeforeEach and @AfterEach Execution",
        "content": "@BeforeEach and @AfterEach execute around each test method invocation. @BeforeEach prepares the conditions required by one test, while @AfterEach releases or resets resources after that invocation. These methods are ideal for creating fresh service objects, initializing test data, configuring mocks, and cleaning temporary files. For example, a repository test can insert one known customer in @BeforeEach, execute a query in the test method, and remove the test data in @AfterEach. This approach keeps each test independent and reduces reliance on execution order. JUnit invokes cleanup callbacks after a test method even when the test fails with an assertion error, although an abruptly terminated process cannot guarantee cleanup. Keep setup focused: if every test requires a large and complicated setup, consider whether the test class contains unrelated responsibilities. Cleanup should be safe when initialization was only partially completed. A null check or a resource handle that records successful initialization can prevent cleanup from causing a second failure. Avoid hiding application defects inside broad exception handling in lifecycle methods. Clear lifecycle methods make failures easier to diagnose."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-5",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — Lifecycle Ordering in Inheritance",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter supports lifecycle methods in test class inheritance hierarchies. A subclass can inherit @BeforeEach, @AfterEach, @BeforeAll, and @AfterAll methods from a superclass when the methods are eligible for inheritance. For the usual class hierarchy, superclass @BeforeEach methods execute before subclass @BeforeEach methods, while subclass @AfterEach methods execute before superclass @AfterEach methods. This ordering allows a base class to establish common infrastructure and a subclass to add specialized preparation. For example, an abstract integration-test base class may configure a shared test environment, while a concrete subclass inserts its own test records. Cleanup should reverse the dependency order so subclass-specific resources are released before shared superclass resources. Overriding methods and using the same method signatures can affect which methods are inherited, so avoid ambiguous lifecycle designs. Do not create a deep hierarchy of lifecycle methods that makes execution difficult to understand. Prefer small, clearly named base classes and inspect JUnit's documented inheritance rules when changing method visibility or overrides. Tests should remain understandable without requiring developers to trace many hidden setup methods."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-6",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — Nested Tests and Callback Ordering",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter supports nested test classes through @Nested. Nested tests allow related scenarios to be grouped inside an outer test class while sharing relevant outer context. Lifecycle callbacks apply at different levels: outer-class lifecycle methods manage the outer test context, and nested-class callbacks manage the nested context. When a nested test executes, the applicable outer and nested setup callbacks run before the test, and cleanup callbacks run afterward according to the documented ordering rules. This is useful for expressing behavior such as an order service with nested groups for a new order, a paid order, and a cancelled order. Each group can have focused setup while reusing common outer configuration. Nested classes are not a reason to place every scenario into one enormous test class. Keep the hierarchy shallow and make the scenario relationship meaningful. Be especially careful with shared mutable fields in outer test instances because nested scenarios may access them. If a nested test modifies outer state, restore it or create independent data for each invocation. Understanding callback ordering helps prevent accidental dependencies between nested scenarios and makes failures easier to localize."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-7",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — Dynamic Tests and Lifecycle Limitations",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter provides dynamic tests through @TestFactory. A test factory generates DynamicTest instances at runtime, which is useful when the number or description of test cases comes from a dataset, configuration, or generated input. Dynamic tests are different from ordinary @Test methods: the generated executable test nodes do not receive the same per-test lifecycle treatment as individual annotated test methods. In particular, @BeforeEach and @AfterEach are associated with the factory method execution rather than being invoked separately around every generated dynamic test. Developers must therefore avoid assuming that each DynamicTest receives a fresh setup and cleanup cycle. If each generated scenario requires independent database state or temporary resources, create and release those resources inside the dynamic test executable or use a suitable parameterized-test design instead. For example, a factory that generates validation cases from a list should ensure that each executable receives independent input and does not mutate shared objects. Dynamic tests are valuable for runtime-generated scenarios, but parameterized tests are often simpler when the test structure is known and only input values vary. Select the model based on the required lifecycle guarantees."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-8",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — Parameterized Test Invocation Lifecycle",
        "content": "Parameterized tests use @ParameterizedTest together with a source such as @ValueSource, @CsvSource, @MethodSource, or another supported argument provider. JUnit creates a separate invocation for each supplied argument set, allowing one test method to verify multiple input cases. Lifecycle callbacks such as @BeforeEach and @AfterEach participate around each parameterized invocation, so per-invocation preparation and cleanup can be applied consistently. For example, a username validation test can receive valid, empty, too-short, and invalid-character inputs from a CSV source. Each invocation should be independently understandable and should not rely on a previous invocation changing shared state. Display names can include argument values to make reports easier to read, but avoid exposing secrets or sensitive test data in output. A @MethodSource provider is useful when inputs require objects or more complex construction. Keep argument providers deterministic so a failing test can be reproduced. If the source reads external data, make its availability and version explicit. Parameterized tests reduce duplication while preserving a clear test lifecycle for each input combination."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-9",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — Test Interfaces and Default Lifecycle Methods",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter allows lifecycle methods and test-related annotations to be declared in interfaces. This capability is useful when multiple test classes need to follow a shared contract without inheriting from one common superclass. An interface can provide default @BeforeEach or @AfterEach methods, and implementing test classes can inherit the applicable default behavior. For example, a repository contract test interface can define common preparation and cleanup while different implementations verify an in-memory repository and a database-backed repository. This promotes reusable tests, but interface lifecycle methods should remain focused and predictable. Avoid multiple interfaces that provide conflicting or duplicated setup responsibilities because the resulting lifecycle becomes difficult to reason about. Keep implementation-specific resources in the implementing class rather than hiding them in a generic interface. Also ensure that shared default methods do not assume fields or dependencies that every implementing class does not provide. A well-designed test interface expresses a reusable behavioral contract and its minimum lifecycle requirements. It should not become a framework for unrelated test configuration."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-10",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — Extensions and Lifecycle Callback APIs",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter extensions provide a supported mechanism for integrating custom behavior into the test lifecycle. Extensions can implement callback interfaces such as BeforeAllCallback, AfterAllCallback, BeforeEachCallback, AfterEachCallback, BeforeTestExecutionCallback, and AfterTestExecutionCallback. These callbacks allow reusable infrastructure to run at specific execution points. For example, a custom extension can record test execution timing, prepare a temporary directory, or manage a test resource. Extensions can be registered declaratively with @ExtendWith or through supported automatic registration mechanisms. The callback chosen matters: a before-each callback and a before-test-execution callback occur at different points in the lifecycle and serve different purposes. Extensions should release resources reliably and avoid introducing hidden dependencies between tests. When an extension stores state, understand its extension context and lifecycle scope so state is not accidentally shared across unrelated test executions. Prefer established extensions for common needs, such as database containers or mocking integrations, instead of writing custom infrastructure unnecessarily. Document custom extension behavior so developers can understand what happens before and after their tests."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-11",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — Resource Management and Exception Handling",
        "content": "Test lifecycle design must account for resources that require explicit cleanup, including files, sockets, database connections, containers, and embedded servers. Allocate a resource at the narrowest scope that can safely support the test. A resource used by one test should normally be created during that test's setup and closed during its cleanup. Java's try-with-resources is often the clearest choice for resources whose lifetime fits inside one method. For resources that must remain available across a complete test invocation, lifecycle methods can manage them, but cleanup must tolerate partial initialization. For example, if a container starts successfully but a later setup step fails, cleanup should still stop the container. Use APIs such as AutoCloseable where possible and preserve the original test failure when cleanup also fails. Avoid swallowing exceptions in @AfterEach because that can hide leaked resources or broken cleanup. Temporary directories and files should use controlled locations and unique names to avoid collisions. In CI, leaked processes or containers can affect later jobs and consume infrastructure. Treat cleanup as part of test correctness, not as optional housekeeping."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-5-section-12",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle — Parallel Execution, Isolation and CI Strategy",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter can execute tests concurrently when parallel execution is enabled through its configuration. Parallel execution can reduce feedback time, but it exposes lifecycle and shared-state problems that sequential execution may hide. A test that writes to a shared file, modifies a static field, uses the same database record, or controls a singleton service may interfere with another test. Prefer independent test data, isolated temporary directories, separate resource instances, and unique identifiers. If a resource cannot safely support concurrent use, apply an appropriate execution mode or synchronization strategy and document why it is required. Do not assume that PER_METHOD automatically makes every external resource isolated; it only creates separate test class instances. CI configuration should make parallel execution settings explicit and consistent between developer machines and build agents. When a test fails intermittently, compare sequential and parallel runs, inspect shared resources, and capture useful logs. A reliable production test pipeline combines deterministic setup, bounded resource usage, cleanup, clear reports, and repeatable execution. Measure whether parallelism improves total build time without reducing test reliability."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Parameterized Tests",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-1",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Understanding Data-Driven Test Execution",
        "content": "Parameterized testing in JUnit 5 allows one test method to execute repeatedly with different input values. Instead of writing separate methods for every input case, developers define a single @ParameterizedTest and supply arguments through an argument source. JUnit creates an individual invocation for each supplied argument set, making the test report show which inputs passed or failed. This approach is useful for validation logic, calculations, configuration parsing, API request handling, and business rules that must behave consistently across many values. For example, an email validation method can be tested with valid addresses, empty strings, malformed addresses, and boundary-length values using one parameterized test. Each invocation should remain independent and should produce a clear assertion result. Parameterized tests reduce duplicated code, but they do not remove the need for meaningful test design. Inputs should represent normal conditions, boundary conditions, invalid values, and important business scenarios. Avoid creating hundreds of meaningless combinations that make reports difficult to understand. A good parameterized test has a focused purpose, readable input data, deterministic execution, and an assertion that clearly explains the expected behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-2",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Required Dependencies and Annotation Configuration",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter parameterized tests require the JUnit Jupiter parameterized-test support module. In Maven projects, developers commonly add the junit-jupiter-params dependency using the same compatible version as the other JUnit Jupiter components. The test method is annotated with @ParameterizedTest rather than ordinary @Test, and an argument source such as @ValueSource or @CsvSource supplies the values. A simplified example is @ParameterizedTest followed by @ValueSource(strings = {\"admin\", \"operator\"}) and a method that accepts one String argument. The JUnit Jupiter engine discovers the parameterized test and creates an invocation for each provided value. The test method parameters must be compatible with the supplied arguments or supported conversions. When using Maven, verify that the test dependency is available on the test classpath and that the Surefire plugin configuration supports the selected JUnit Platform version. Version mismatches between the API, engine, and parameterized-test module can produce discovery or execution errors. Keep dependency versions aligned through dependency management or a consistent version property. After configuration, run the test through Maven and confirm that the report contains separate invocations for the supplied inputs."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-3",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Using @ValueSource for Single-Argument Cases",
        "content": "@ValueSource is one of the simplest argument sources in JUnit Jupiter. It supplies a collection of literal values to a parameterized test and is suitable when each invocation needs one argument. Supported value types include common primitives and their wrappers, String, and Class values. For example, a username policy test can use @ValueSource(strings = {\"devops\", \"release-user\", \"build-agent\"}) and verify that each supplied name satisfies a rule. Numeric tests can use integer, long, double, or other supported value arrays where appropriate. The test method declares a parameter whose type matches the supplied value or can be handled by supported conversion. @ValueSource is not intended for supplying several unrelated arguments to one invocation; use CSV or a method source when multiple parameters are required. Also remember that an empty source array does not provide useful test coverage. Choose values that represent meaningful behavior rather than arbitrary samples. When testing boundary rules, include values immediately below, at, and above the relevant boundary. A readable @ValueSource test keeps the input list close to the behavior being verified and makes simple cases easy to maintain."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-4",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Null, Empty and Combined Argument Sources",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter provides specialized argument sources for null and empty input conditions. @NullSource supplies a null argument, while @EmptySource supplies an empty value for supported types such as String and supported collection or array types. @NullAndEmptySource combines both cases and is useful for testing methods that must reject missing or empty input. These annotations can be combined with another source annotation when the test must cover both invalid and ordinary values. For example, a configuration-key validator may need to reject null, an empty string, and whitespace-only values while accepting a valid key. Null and empty are not equivalent: null means no object reference, whereas an empty string or collection is an existing object containing no elements. Tests should assert the intended business behavior separately where the distinction matters. Do not assume @EmptySource automatically supplies whitespace-only strings; add explicit whitespace values through another source. These source annotations are particularly valuable for defensive programming, API validation, and repository methods. Their use makes missing-input behavior visible in test reports instead of hiding it inside a large collection of unrelated test cases."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-5",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — CSV Arguments with @CsvSource",
        "content": "@CsvSource supplies multiple arguments to each parameterized-test invocation using comma-separated records. Each record represents one invocation, and each field maps to a test method parameter. This is useful for testing relationships between inputs and expected outputs, such as a price calculation, role-to-permission mapping, or string transformation. For example, records can provide an input username and its expected normalized value. Developers should pay attention to quoting, delimiters, empty fields, and special characters because CSV parsing rules affect the values received by the method. The default delimiter is a comma, but annotation options allow the delimiter and other parsing behavior to be configured. A value representing an empty string is different from a null value, and special null-value configuration should be used deliberately. Keep records short enough to understand and avoid placing complex structured objects into annotation strings. When the data becomes large or difficult to read, move it into a method source or an external test-data mechanism. Always verify that each CSV field maps to the intended parameter and that expected results are asserted explicitly."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-6",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Enum, Method and Field Argument Sources",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter provides argument sources for values that are more complex than simple literals. @EnumSource supplies enum constants and can select or exclude specific constants, making it useful for verifying behavior across states such as PENDING, APPROVED, REJECTED, and CANCELLED. @MethodSource obtains arguments from a factory method and is suitable when test inputs require object construction or multiple arguments. A method source can return streams, collections, or other supported argument-provider structures. The factory method must follow JUnit's supported naming and visibility rules, and its return values must match the test method's parameter structure. @FieldSource is available in supported JUnit Jupiter versions for sourcing arguments from fields, which can be useful for reusable static test data. Check the project's actual JUnit version before using version-specific annotations. For example, a payment test can use a method source to create payment requests with different currencies and expected outcomes. Keep data providers deterministic, avoid external network calls during argument generation, and use descriptive factory names. These sources improve maintainability when data needs more structure than annotation literals can comfortably express."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-7",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Building Arguments with Arguments and ArgumentsAccessor",
        "content": "When a parameterized test needs multiple values per invocation, JUnit Jupiter can represent each record with Arguments. A method source can return a stream of Arguments objects, where each object contains the values supplied to one test invocation. For example, a tax calculation test may provide the original amount, tax rate, and expected final amount in each Arguments record. The test method can declare three strongly typed parameters, making the relationship between the data and assertions easy to read. ArgumentsAccessor provides an alternative for accessing indexed or typed values from a grouped argument set, but direct method parameters are usually clearer for ordinary test cases. If a record contains many fields, consider using a dedicated test-data object or an argument aggregator rather than relying on unexplained numeric positions. Validate test-data construction so incorrect values do not silently produce misleading test results. Keep expected values independent from the production calculation where possible; calculating the expected result by calling the same production method would make the assertion meaningless. Structured arguments make complex business-rule tests easier to expand and review."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-8",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Custom Argument Providers and ArgumentsProvider",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter allows developers to create custom argument providers when built-in sources do not express the required test data clearly. A custom provider implements the ArgumentsProvider interface and supplies a stream of Arguments through its provideArguments method. It can be registered with @ArgumentsSource on a parameterized test. This approach is useful when many test classes need the same carefully constructed scenario set or when test inputs are generated from a controlled rule. For example, a custom provider may generate boundary cases for a date-range validator, including the first allowed date, last allowed date, and values outside the range. Providers should remain deterministic and should not depend on production services or unstable external systems. If a provider reads a file or configuration resource, make the dependency explicit and report a clear error when the resource is unavailable. Avoid custom providers for simple lists that @ValueSource or @CsvSource can already handle. Too much provider abstraction can make test data harder to discover. Use custom argument providers when they create genuine reuse or improve the clarity of complex input generation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-9",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Implicit Conversion, Explicit Conversion and Aggregation",
        "content": "Parameterized test arguments may arrive in a different representation from the type expected by the test method. JUnit Jupiter supports implicit conversions for many common types, such as converting suitable strings into primitive or wrapper values. For more specialized requirements, developers can use explicit argument conversion mechanisms such as an ArgumentConverter registered with @ConvertWith. For example, a test source may provide a string representing a deployment environment, while a custom converter creates a strongly typed Environment object. When one invocation contains several related values, an ArgumentsAggregator can combine them into a domain-specific object. This can make a test method easier to understand because it receives one meaningful object instead of many positional parameters. Converters and aggregators should validate their input and provide clear exceptions when data is malformed. Do not hide complex business behavior inside a converter; its responsibility is test-argument transformation. Prefer built-in conversion when it is sufficient, and introduce custom conversion only when it improves clarity or supports a real type requirement. Clear conversion boundaries help prevent invalid test data from producing confusing failures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-10",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Display Names, Placeholders and Readable Reports",
        "content": "Parameterized tests create multiple invocations, so meaningful display names are important for identifying failures. JUnit Jupiter supports display-name patterns and placeholders that can include invocation indexes, argument values, and other supported metadata. Developers can configure a pattern through the @ParameterizedTest annotation or use a display-name customization mechanism where appropriate. For example, a validation test can produce names that show the invocation number and supplied username, allowing a failed case to be identified without opening the source code. Display names should be concise, readable, and safe for CI logs. Do not include passwords, access tokens, personal data, or confidential production information in test arguments if those values may appear in reports. When arguments are complex objects, provide useful string representations or choose a custom naming approach that exposes only relevant fields. Good display names reduce debugging time because the failing scenario becomes immediately visible in IDE and build reports. Avoid overly long output that makes reports difficult to scan. Consistent naming conventions help teams compare test results across local execution and CI pipelines."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-11",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Lifecycle, Repeated Invocations and Test Isolation",
        "content": "Each parameterized invocation participates in the JUnit test execution lifecycle. Lifecycle callbacks such as @BeforeEach and @AfterEach run around individual invocations, allowing setup and cleanup to be performed for each argument set. This is important when one input changes database state, creates a temporary file, or configures a mock. Each invocation should start from a known state and should not rely on values changed by a previous invocation. For example, a parameterized repository test can create a fresh record for each supplied status and remove it afterward. A test class using PER_CLASS lifecycle mode must be especially careful with mutable instance fields because the same test object can be reused. Parallel execution can introduce additional interference if invocations share external resources. Use unique identifiers, isolated test data, and controlled cleanup. Do not assume that parameterized invocations always execute in a particular order. If order matters to a business workflow, model the workflow as a dedicated integration test instead of depending on argument order. Isolation makes parameterized tests reliable both locally and on shared CI agents."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-12",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Boundary Testing, Equivalence Classes and Data Quality",
        "content": "Parameterized tests are especially effective when input data is selected using systematic test-design techniques. Equivalence partitioning divides inputs into groups expected to behave similarly, while boundary-value analysis focuses on values at and around important limits. For a password-length rule requiring between 8 and 64 characters, representative cases should include lengths below the minimum, exactly the minimum, an ordinary valid length, exactly the maximum, and above the maximum. A parameterized test can execute these cases consistently and show which boundary failed. The test data should also cover null, empty, malformed, and unusual values when those conditions are part of the contract. Avoid choosing only successful examples because that leaves rejection behavior untested. Expected results should be stated explicitly for each input. If the data set becomes very large, divide it into focused test methods so each group has a clear purpose. Review test data when business requirements change, because old boundaries may no longer represent the current contract. Well-designed input sets provide stronger coverage than simply increasing the number of invocations."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-6-section-13",
        "title": "Parameterized Tests — Maven, CI Execution and Failure Diagnosis",
        "content": "Parameterized tests run through the same JUnit Platform infrastructure used by ordinary Jupiter tests, so Maven Surefire can execute them as part of the test phase when dependencies and plugin configuration are compatible. A developer can run the project tests with mvn test and inspect the generated reports to identify failed invocations. In a CI pipeline, publish test reports even when the test step fails so the team can review the exact input that caused the failure. A useful report should identify the test method, invocation display name, assertion message, and stack trace. When a parameterized test fails only on a build agent, compare Java and JUnit versions, locale, timezone, environment variables, file paths, and test-data availability. Do not automatically rerun every failure without investigating whether it is deterministic or caused by shared state. Keep generated test data stable and avoid relying on external services unless the test is explicitly an integration test. CI jobs should preserve reports as artifacts and make failure information visible to developers. This turns parameterized testing into a repeatable quality gate rather than a collection of local-only checks."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Mockito Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-1",
        "title": "Mockito's Role in Java Unit Testing",
        "content": "Mockito is a Java mocking framework used to isolate the class under test from its external dependencies. In a typical application, a service may depend on repositories, HTTP clients, message publishers, payment gateways, or other services. Testing the service with all these real dependencies can make tests slow, unreliable, and difficult to control. Mockito allows a test to replace selected dependencies with mock objects whose behavior is controlled by the test. This helps developers verify business logic independently from databases, networks, and infrastructure. Mockito does not replace JUnit; JUnit commonly manages test execution while Mockito supplies mock behavior and interaction verification. For example, an OrderService test can mock OrderRepository and PaymentClient, configure the expected responses, invoke the service, and verify that the correct payment request was made. A strong unit test still focuses on observable behavior rather than simply proving that Mockito APIs were called. The goal is reliable feedback about application logic, not maximizing the number of mocks."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-2",
        "title": "Mockito Dependency and Project Setup",
        "content": "Mockito must be available on the test classpath before a Java test can use its APIs. In Maven projects, developers normally add mockito-core as a test-scoped dependency and use a compatible version aligned with the project's Java and testing framework requirements. Gradle projects declare the dependency using testImplementation. Mockito's JUnit Jupiter integration is commonly provided by mockito-junit-jupiter, which supplies MockitoExtension for JUnit 5. Dependency versions should be managed consistently rather than mixing incompatible Mockito artifacts. After adding the dependency, the build tool downloads the required libraries and makes them available during test compilation and execution without packaging them into the production application. Teams should inspect dependency trees when an unexpected Mockito version is loaded transitively. A clean build is useful for confirming that tests do not depend on an IDE-only library. In CI, dependency resolution should use approved repositories and reproducible version management so developers and build agents execute the same test framework."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-3",
        "title": "Mock Objects, Stubs, and Spies",
        "content": "A mock is a test double that allows a test to define responses and inspect interactions with a dependency. A stub provides predetermined answers to calls, while a mock can additionally support verification that particular interactions occurred. A spy wraps or observes a real object and can selectively replace method behavior, although it still retains real behavior for methods that are not stubbed. These terms describe different testing purposes and should not be treated as interchangeable. For example, a mocked inventory client may return an available quantity without contacting a real inventory system. A stubbed repository response can provide a known customer record. A spy around a formatter may allow the test to observe selected calls while retaining its actual implementation. Prefer ordinary mocks for external boundaries and real objects for simple domain logic. Spies require additional care because invoking real methods can trigger filesystem, network, or other side effects. Choosing the correct test double keeps tests understandable and avoids accidentally testing infrastructure instead of business behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-4",
        "title": "Creating Mocks with Mockito APIs",
        "content": "Mockito provides several ways to create mock objects. The Mockito.mock method creates a mock for a specified class or interface, while annotation-based declarations can make test classes easier to read. With JUnit 5, MockitoExtension can initialize fields annotated with @Mock and inject them where appropriate. A mock represents a controlled test double, not a real implementation of the dependency. The test should create only the mocks needed for the behavior under examination. For example, an InvoiceService test might declare a mocked InvoiceRepository and a mocked TaxClient, then pass them to the service constructor. Constructor-based setup makes dependencies explicit and avoids relying on hidden field injection. Mockito can also create mocks programmatically when a test needs different mock configurations. Developers should avoid creating unnecessary mocks for simple value objects or domain classes whose real behavior is inexpensive and deterministic. A clear arrangement of mocks helps readers understand which external interactions the test controls."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-5",
        "title": "Stubbing Methods with when and thenReturn",
        "content": "Stubbing tells a mock how to respond when the test invokes a particular method. Mockito commonly uses when(mock.method(arguments)).thenReturn(value) for methods that return values. This lets the test create predictable conditions without depending on a real database or remote service. For example, when customerRepository.findById(10L) is called, the mock can return a prepared customer object. The service then processes that object using its real business logic. Stubbing should represent meaningful scenarios such as an existing record, a missing record, an available resource, or a rejected request. Avoid configuring every possible method call without a reason, because excessive setup makes tests difficult to maintain. Mockito also supports consecutive return values and alternative answers for more complex behavior. A good test keeps its stubbing close to the scenario it describes and verifies the externally observable result after the system under test executes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-6",
        "title": "Argument Matchers and Flexible Stubbing",
        "content": "Argument matchers allow Mockito stubbing and verification to work with broader argument conditions instead of requiring one exact value. Common matchers include any(), anyString(), eq(), isNull(), and argThat(). They are useful when a method receives generated identifiers, timestamps, or objects whose complete values are not important to the test. For example, a notification client may be expected to receive any non-null message object, while a specific destination address must match an exact value. Mockito requires consistent matcher usage within a method invocation: when one argument uses a matcher, the other arguments generally must also be supplied through matchers such as eq(). Mixing raw values and matchers incorrectly can cause InvalidUseOfMatchersException. Matchers should not be used merely to make assertions less precise. If an exact argument is part of the business requirement, assert that exact value. Custom argument matchers should express meaningful conditions and remain readable so failures are easy to diagnose."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-7",
        "title": "Verifying Interactions and Invocation Counts",
        "content": "Mockito verification checks whether a mock received an expected interaction during test execution. The verify method can confirm that a dependency method was called, and verification modes such as times(), never(), atLeastOnce(), and atMost() control the expected invocation count. For example, an order-processing test can verify that a payment client was called once after a valid order was submitted. It can also verify that a failed validation did not trigger a payment request. Interaction verification is valuable when the collaboration between objects is part of the behavior being tested, such as sending a message after a successful transaction. However, verifying every internal method call creates brittle tests that fail after harmless implementation refactoring. Prefer assertions on returned values, persisted outcomes, or externally meaningful effects. Use never() when the absence of a dangerous action is important. Verification should communicate a requirement, not duplicate every line of the implementation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-8",
        "title": "Capturing Arguments for Detailed Assertions",
        "content": "ArgumentCaptor captures the actual argument passed to a mock so that a test can inspect its fields after execution. This is useful when the system under test constructs a complex command, event, notification, or request object internally. For example, an OrderService may create an OrderCreatedEvent and pass it to an event publisher. The test can verify that the publisher received the event, capture the argument, and assert the order identifier, customer details, and event metadata. Captors are normally declared with @Captor or created through ArgumentCaptor.forClass. They should be used when the argument's content matters more than simply confirming that a method was called. A captor is not a replacement for a direct assertion on a returned object, and it should not be introduced when a simple eq() matcher is sufficient. Keep captured-argument assertions focused on business-relevant fields so changes to irrelevant implementation details do not unnecessarily break tests."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-9",
        "title": "Mockito Annotations and MockitoExtension",
        "content": "Mockito annotations reduce repetitive mock initialization in test classes. @Mock declares a mock dependency, @Spy declares a spy, @Captor declares an argument captor, and @InjectMocks asks Mockito to create the object under test and inject available mock dependencies. In JUnit 5, MockitoExtension is commonly registered with @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class), allowing Mockito annotations to be initialized for each test lifecycle. This integration avoids manually opening Mockito annotations in every test. Developers should understand that @InjectMocks is a convenience mechanism rather than a dependency-injection container. If construction is ambiguous or dependencies are not injected as expected, explicit constructor setup is often clearer. A test should make the system under test and its dependencies easy to identify. Annotation-based setup is particularly useful for consistent test classes, but too many annotations can hide important construction details. Teams should select one initialization approach consistently and avoid combining multiple lifecycle mechanisms without understanding their interaction."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-10",
        "title": "Testing Exceptions and Failure Paths",
        "content": "Mockito can simulate dependency failures so tests can verify how application code handles exceptional conditions. The thenThrow method configures a mock to throw an exception when a particular method is invoked. This helps test database outages, rejected remote requests, unavailable services, and other failure paths without causing real infrastructure failures. For example, a repository mock can throw a data-access exception, and the service test can verify whether the application translates it into a domain-specific exception or propagates it correctly. JUnit assertions such as assertThrows should normally verify the exception visible to the caller. The test can also check that cleanup or compensation behavior occurred when required. Mockito's doThrow syntax is useful for void methods and certain spy scenarios. Avoid asserting only that some exception occurred if the exception type and meaning are important. Failure-path tests should document expected application behavior and ensure that errors are not silently swallowed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-11",
        "title": "Mocking Void Methods and Callback Behavior",
        "content": "Methods returning void cannot use the ordinary when(...).thenReturn(...) pattern because there is no return value to configure. Mockito provides doNothing(), doThrow(), and doAnswer() for such cases. A void method may represent publishing an event, sending a notification, deleting a record, or writing to an external system. By default, a standard mock does nothing for a void invocation, so explicit doNothing() is often unnecessary. doThrow() is useful when a test must simulate an exception from a void dependency method. doAnswer() provides access to invocation arguments and can model a callback or capture information when a method is called. For example, a mocked asynchronous client may accept a callback that the test needs to invoke with a controlled response. Such tests should keep callback behavior deterministic and avoid introducing real threads or timing dependencies unless concurrency is the subject of the test."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-12",
        "title": "Strict Stubbing and Unnecessary Interactions",
        "content": "Strict stubbing helps detect test setup that does not match actual execution. Mockito's strictness support can report unused stubbings and argument mismatches, which often reveal incorrect test assumptions or unnecessary configuration. An unused stub may indicate that the test no longer exercises the intended path after application behavior changed. An argument mismatch may mean the service passed a different identifier or request than the test expected. These checks improve test quality by exposing stale setup rather than allowing it to remain unnoticed. Developers should normally correct the setup or the test scenario instead of immediately marking stubbings lenient. Lenient stubbing has a place when shared setup legitimately supports multiple scenarios, but excessive leniency hides mistakes. Keep common setup limited and move scenario-specific stubbing into individual tests. Strictness is most useful when failures are treated as diagnostic feedback and resolved with clear intent."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-13",
        "title": "Mocking Final, Static, and Construction Behavior",
        "content": "Modern Mockito versions can support mocking final classes and methods, static methods, and constructor behavior through its inline mocking capabilities, subject to the project's Mockito version and runtime configuration. Static mocking is sometimes useful when legacy code calls global utilities directly, while construction mocking can help isolate code that creates dependencies internally. These techniques should be applied carefully because they can conceal tightly coupled design and make tests harder to understand. A static mock generally has a scoped lifetime and must be closed after use so it does not affect unrelated tests. Constructor mocking can be useful during incremental modernization, but dependency injection is usually a cleaner long-term design. Before using advanced mocking features, verify the configured mock maker, Java runtime compatibility, and build dependencies. Prefer refactoring application code to expose dependencies explicitly when practical, and reserve advanced mocking for cases where simpler isolation is not feasible."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-14",
        "title": "Testing Asynchronous and Concurrent Collaborations",
        "content": "Asynchronous code introduces additional testing concerns because a method may schedule work that completes after the original call returns. Mockito can verify that an asynchronous dependency was invoked and can capture callbacks or submitted tasks for controlled execution. For example, a service may submit a Runnable to an executor; a test can capture the submitted task and run it synchronously to avoid unpredictable scheduling. When testing callback-based APIs, invoke the captured callback with success and failure responses and assert the resulting application behavior. Avoid arbitrary Thread.sleep calls because they make tests slow and timing-sensitive. For genuine concurrent behavior, use synchronization primitives, bounded waiting, and deterministic test coordination. Mockito verification with timeout() can be useful in selected cases, but it should not become a substitute for sound synchronization. Tests should distinguish between verifying that work was scheduled and verifying that the asynchronous result was processed correctly."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-7-section-15",
        "title": "Mockito Test Design, Isolation, and Maintainability",
        "content": "A maintainable Mockito test has a clear scenario, a focused system under test, controlled dependency behavior, and assertions that describe expected outcomes. The arrange-act-assert pattern provides a readable structure: arrange mocks and input data, act by invoking the real application logic, and assert the result or meaningful interactions. Each test should be independent so its outcome does not depend on execution order or shared mutable mock state. Avoid overspecifying interactions, mocking simple value objects, or creating mocks for every class in the dependency graph. Excessive mocking can produce tests that pass even when real components are incompatible. Use realistic domain data and name tests according to the behavior they protect. When many tests repeat complex setup, introduce small reusable fixtures without hiding the scenario. A healthy test suite balances isolated unit tests with integration tests that validate actual framework and infrastructure wiring.\n\nCI PIPELINE EXECUTION AND REPORTING\n\nMockito tests are normally executed through the project's standard Java test lifecycle. Maven projects use the test phase, commonly through Maven Surefire for unit tests, while Gradle projects use the test task. A CI pipeline should check out the correct source revision, configure the required JDK, resolve dependencies, compile the application, and execute the test suite. Test reports should be published so developers can inspect failures without reproducing every build locally. Mockito itself does not provide the build pipeline; it runs as part of the JUnit or other test framework execution. Teams should keep test dependencies scoped correctly, pin compatible versions, and avoid relying on developer workstation state. If tests pass locally but fail in CI, inspect Java versions, dependency resolution, parallel execution, environment variables, and test isolation. A reliable pipeline makes unit-test failures visible early and prevents broken changes from progressing toward deployment."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Mocking Dependencies",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-1",
        "title": "Dependency Boundaries and Isolation Strategy",
        "content": "Dependency mocking begins by identifying the boundaries between the class under test and the components it depends on. A service may communicate with repositories, HTTP clients, message brokers, payment gateways, email providers, or cloud APIs. These dependencies introduce external behavior that can make a unit test slow, expensive, or unpredictable. Mockito allows a test to replace selected collaborators with controlled objects while keeping the service's own business logic real. The important design decision is choosing which boundary to isolate. Mocking every object in an application can create tests that verify implementation details without validating meaningful behavior. A useful approach is to mock dependencies that cross process, infrastructure, or independently owned component boundaries, while using real simple domain objects. For example, an OrderService can use a real Order and mocked OrderRepository and PaymentGateway. This arrangement lets the test verify order-processing rules without requiring a database or an actual payment provider."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-2",
        "title": "Constructor Injection and Explicit Dependency Wiring",
        "content": "Constructor injection makes dependencies visible and allows tests to supply Mockito mocks directly. A service that requires CustomerRepository and NotificationClient should receive both through its constructor rather than locating them through global state. The unit test can create each mock, construct the service, configure expected responses, and invoke the real service method. This makes the test's dependency graph easy to understand and avoids hidden initialization. Constructor injection also helps production applications validate that required dependencies are present when objects are created. Mockito's @InjectMocks can perform convenient injection, but it should not hide complicated object construction or replace a clear constructor-based setup. If a class has many dependencies, that may indicate excessive responsibility and an opportunity to split the design. Explicit wiring also helps diagnose missing mocks and unexpected null references. In larger systems, dependency injection frameworks can assemble production objects while unit tests create a smaller, controlled object graph without starting the entire application context."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-3",
        "title": "Mocking Repository and Data Access Dependencies",
        "content": "Repository dependencies represent the persistence boundary between business logic and stored data. A unit test can mock a repository to return a known entity, an empty result, or a simulated persistence exception. For example, a UserService test can configure findByEmail to return an existing user and verify that duplicate registration is rejected. Another test can return an empty result and verify the registration path. Mockito makes these scenarios repeatable without starting a database. However, mocked repository tests do not prove that SQL queries, ORM mappings, transactions, or database constraints work correctly. Those concerns need integration tests against a real or containerized database. Keep repository mock behavior aligned with the repository contract, including optional results and exception semantics. Avoid returning impossible data that a real database could never produce. This separation gives fast feedback for service rules while preserving integration coverage for persistence behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-4",
        "title": "Mocking REST Clients and External HTTP Services",
        "content": "A REST client dependency communicates with another application over HTTP and may encounter latency, timeouts, authentication errors, invalid responses, or service unavailability. Mockito can isolate the application service from the HTTP client by returning controlled response objects or throwing configured exceptions. For example, an exchange-rate service can receive a mocked currency client response and calculate a conversion without contacting a public API. Tests should cover successful responses, missing data, rejected requests, and translated client exceptions. Verify important request parameters when they represent business requirements, such as the destination account or resource identifier. A Mockito test does not validate URL construction, HTTP serialization, TLS configuration, or the behavior of the actual client library. Those require focused client integration or contract tests. Mock at the client abstraction when testing business logic, and use a real HTTP test server or equivalent integration setup when validating transport-level behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-5",
        "title": "Mocking Message Brokers and Event Publishers",
        "content": "Message publishers and broker clients are infrastructure dependencies that can be isolated with Mockito when testing application decisions. A service may publish an OrderCreated event after an order is accepted, or send a notification after a payment succeeds. A mock publisher allows the test to verify that the expected event was submitted with the correct business information. It can also prove that no event was published when validation failed. ArgumentCaptor is useful for inspecting event fields such as aggregate identifier, event type, and relevant payload values. However, verifying a publisher method call does not prove that a broker accepted the message, persisted it, delivered it, or preserved ordering. Those behaviors need integration tests with the actual broker or a suitable test environment. Keep the unit test focused on the decision to publish and the event content. Use separate integration coverage for serialization, routing keys, acknowledgements, retries, and consumer compatibility."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-6",
        "title": "Mocking Payment Gateways and High-Risk Integrations",
        "content": "Payment gateways and other high-risk integrations should be isolated in unit tests because real calls can move money, create charges, or trigger irreversible external actions. A mocked payment client can return approved, declined, pending, or failed outcomes so the service's business rules can be tested safely. For example, an order service may mark an order as paid only after receiving an approved payment result. A separate test can simulate a timeout and verify that the application does not incorrectly assume the payment failed or succeeded without reconciliation. Verify critical request details such as amount, currency, idempotency key, and merchant reference when they are part of the contract. Do not use production credentials or real payment endpoints in unit tests. Mockito cannot prove the provider's real behavior, so integration and sandbox contract tests should validate request compatibility separately. Sensitive data should never be placed in test logs or captured arguments unnecessarily."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-7",
        "title": "Mocking Email, SMS, and Notification Providers",
        "content": "Notification providers send messages through email, SMS, push, or other external delivery systems. These integrations can be mocked to verify when a notification should be requested and which recipient or template should be used. For example, a password-reset service can be tested by supplying a mocked email sender and verifying that a reset notification is requested only after a valid token is generated. A test can also simulate provider rejection or an exception and verify the application's retry or error-handling policy. Capturing the notification request allows assertions on recipient, template identifier, and non-sensitive metadata. Avoid asserting every internal formatting detail if a dedicated template test covers it. A mocked sender does not prove actual delivery, spam filtering, provider acceptance, or message rendering in a real inbox. Those concerns need provider sandbox tests or specialized integration checks. Unit tests should remain safe, deterministic, and free from real customer communication."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-8",
        "title": "Mocking Time, Clock, and Random Dependencies",
        "content": "Time and randomness can make tests difficult to reproduce when application code reads the system clock or generates random values directly. Java's Clock abstraction allows a service to receive a controllable time source, and Mockito can mock a Clock when a test needs a specific instant. A fixed clock is often even simpler because it provides deterministic time without requiring stubbing every clock method. This is useful for token expiry, subscription renewal, billing periods, retention policies, and scheduled workflows. Random generators can similarly be injected so tests can control generated values. For example, a token service test can verify that a token expires exactly fifteen minutes after a fixed creation instant. Avoid tests that depend on the real current time or narrow timing windows. Mockito can isolate these dependencies, but production design should prefer explicit abstractions over static calls to system time. Deterministic inputs make failures easier to reproduce locally and in CI."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-9",
        "title": "Mocking Filesystem and Operating System Services",
        "content": "Filesystem and operating-system interactions include reading files, writing reports, checking directories, launching processes, and accessing environment-specific resources. A service that directly uses static file APIs can be difficult to unit test because it depends on the machine's filesystem state. Introducing a FileStorage or FileSystemGateway interface creates a boundary that Mockito can control. Tests can simulate an existing file, a missing file, permission denial, or an I/O exception without changing real system files. For example, a configuration import service can receive a mocked storage dependency and verify how it handles an unavailable configuration document. Be careful not to mock simple path or value objects that are already deterministic. Mockito does not validate actual file permissions, filesystem semantics, process exit codes, or operating-system differences. Use temporary directories and integration tests for those behaviors. Keep test fixtures isolated so parallel builds do not overwrite shared files."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-10",
        "title": "Mocking Cache and Distributed Cache Dependencies",
        "content": "Cache dependencies such as Redis clients or application cache abstractions influence how data is retrieved and stored. A mocked cache can simulate cache hits, cache misses, failed reads, and write operations while testing service decisions. For example, a product service may return a cached product when available and query the repository when the cache returns no value. Mockito can verify that the repository is not unnecessarily called during a cache hit and that the cache is updated after a successful data change. These tests should reflect the cache abstraction's contract, including expiration and null-handling rules where applicable. Mocking a cache does not validate serialization, eviction policies, distributed consistency, cluster connectivity, or actual Redis commands. Those behaviors require integration tests with the real cache technology. Avoid making unit tests depend on assumptions about cache internals when the application only relies on a small interface. Test business decisions in isolation and infrastructure behavior at the appropriate integration boundary."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-11",
        "title": "Mocking Authentication and Authorization Providers",
        "content": "Authentication and authorization dependencies determine who a caller is and which operations are permitted. A unit test can mock a token validator, identity provider adapter, or authorization service to provide controlled user identities and permission decisions. For example, an account service may proceed when an authorization dependency confirms that the caller owns the account, and reject the request when access is denied. Tests should cover missing identities, expired or invalid credentials, insufficient roles, and dependency failures. Avoid using Mockito to bypass security checks in a way that makes the application appear secure without exercising its actual security configuration. A mocked authorization decision only tests how the service responds to that decision; it does not validate token signatures, cryptographic algorithms, issuer configuration, or HTTP security filters. Those require dedicated security integration tests. Never place real tokens, passwords, or secrets in mock setup or diagnostic output."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-12",
        "title": "Mocking Third-Party SDKs and Cloud Service Clients",
        "content": "Third-party SDKs and cloud clients often expose large APIs and may perform network operations, retries, credential discovery, or background work. Directly mocking a large SDK throughout an application couples unit tests to vendor-specific classes and makes upgrades harder. A cleaner design is to introduce a small application-owned adapter that exposes only the operations the business service needs. Mockito can mock that adapter while the production implementation delegates to the cloud SDK. For example, a document service may depend on ObjectStorageGateway rather than an entire cloud storage client. Unit tests can then simulate successful uploads, missing objects, and provider failures. Separate adapter integration tests verify the actual SDK configuration, request mapping, authentication, and endpoint behavior. This approach reduces mocking complexity and protects business logic from vendor API changes. Keep adapter interfaces meaningful and avoid creating one-method wrappers without a clear boundary or testing benefit."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-13",
        "title": "Handling Dependency Timeouts, Retries, and Circuit Breakers",
        "content": "Distributed dependencies can fail through timeouts, temporary unavailability, throttling, or network interruption. Mockito allows tests to simulate these conditions by throwing timeout exceptions or returning controlled responses on successive calls. A retry policy test can configure a first-call failure followed by a successful response and verify the expected number of attempts. A circuit-breaker test can verify how the service behaves when the dependency is considered unavailable. These tests should validate observable policy behavior such as fallback results, retry limits, and error translation rather than asserting unrelated internal method calls. Be careful when the retry mechanism is implemented by a framework interceptor because a direct unit test may bypass the proxy that activates it. In that case, a framework-level integration test is required. Mockito can control the dependency response, but it does not prove real network timing, distributed failure detection, or production resilience under load."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-14",
        "title": "Avoiding Over-Mocking and Preserving Real Behavior",
        "content": "Over-mocking occurs when tests replace too many collaborators with mocks, including objects whose real behavior is simple, deterministic, and important to the scenario. Such tests can pass even when the real components do not work together. For example, mocking a domain calculation object may cause the test to skip the calculation that the service is supposed to perform. Excessive mocks also create long setup blocks and fragile interaction expectations. Prefer real value objects, simple domain services, and deterministic utility classes when they do not introduce external side effects. Mock external systems and boundaries where control is genuinely needed. If constructing the object graph requires many mocks, review whether the class has too many responsibilities or depends on overly broad interfaces. A useful test should preserve the real behavior that it intends to validate. The objective is not the largest possible number of mocks, but a clear and reliable test of the chosen unit's responsibility."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-15",
        "title": "Mocking Dependency Failures and Recovery Paths",
        "content": "Dependency failure tests verify that application code responds correctly when a collaborator cannot complete its operation. Mockito can simulate exceptions, empty results, rejected requests, and unexpected responses. A service may need to translate a low-level database exception into a domain exception, return a controlled fallback, or initiate a compensating action. Tests should cover both the immediate failure and any required recovery behavior. For example, if an inventory reservation fails after an order has been created, the test can verify that the service requests cancellation or marks the workflow for reconciliation. Avoid asserting only that an exception was thrown when the important requirement is preventing an inconsistent business state. Also test that success-only actions, such as publishing a completion event, do not occur after failure. Recovery behavior should be explicit and deterministic. For workflows involving multiple external systems, unit tests should be complemented by integration tests that validate transaction boundaries and real compensation mechanisms."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-8-section-16",
        "title": "Dependency Contract Testing, Integration Boundaries, and CI",
        "content": "Mockito-based unit tests validate how a class behaves when dependencies return controlled responses, but they do not guarantee that the real implementation satisfies the expected contract. A service may expect a repository to return Optional.empty for a missing record while the actual adapter throws a different exception. Contract and integration tests help detect such mismatches. A contract test can verify request and response formats between independently developed services, while an integration test can validate an adapter against a real database, HTTP test server, or message broker. Keep these tests separate from fast unit tests so the build clearly communicates which boundary is being validated. Mockito may still be useful inside integration tests for unrelated collaborators, but replacing the component under test defeats the purpose of the integration check. A balanced strategy combines isolated service tests, adapter contract tests, and a smaller number of end-to-end workflows. This gives fast feedback without creating false confidence from mocks alone.\n\nPRODUCTION-GRADE CI EXECUTION\n\nA production-grade test suite uses dependency mocks to make unit tests fast and deterministic while retaining separate validation of real integrations. CI should execute unit tests on every relevant change, collect test reports, and clearly identify failures caused by application logic, mock configuration, or environment differences. Tests should not call production services, require developer credentials, or depend on a shared mutable external resource. Use stable test data and explicit dependency versions so the same test behavior is reproduced on developer machines and build agents. When a Mockito test fails, inspect the configured stubbing, actual arguments, invocation counts, exception paths, and whether the system under test received the intended mock. Strict stubbing can expose unused setup and argument mismatches. Teams should review tests for excessive mocking, brittle verification, and missing integration coverage. The goal is a reliable feedback system that catches business logic defects early while separately checking that real infrastructure adapters work correctly."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Service Unit Testing",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-1",
        "title": "Service Layer Responsibilities and Unit Testing Boundaries",
        "content": "The service layer usually coordinates application use cases by applying business rules and communicating with repositories, external gateways, and other application components. Service unit testing verifies this orchestration without requiring the entire application infrastructure to run. A service test should execute the real service implementation while replacing selected external collaborators with controlled test doubles. For example, an OrderService may validate an order, calculate its total, request inventory reservation, save the order, and publish an event. A focused unit test checks the service's decisions and the order in which meaningful operations occur where ordering is part of the requirement. It should not attempt to prove that the database driver, HTTP client, or message broker works. Those responsibilities belong to integration tests. Clearly defining the service boundary prevents tests from becoming either too broad or too shallow. It also helps teams identify which behavior belongs in a service, which belongs in a domain object, and which belongs in an infrastructure adapter."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-2",
        "title": "Service Test Structure Using Arrange Act Assert",
        "content": "Arrange Act Assert is a simple structure for writing readable service unit tests. During Arrange, the test prepares input data, creates the service dependencies, and configures mock responses. During Act, it invokes the service method once using the scenario being tested. During Assert, it checks the returned result, thrown exception, state change, or meaningful collaboration with dependencies. For example, a createCustomer test can arrange a valid registration request and an empty repository lookup, act by calling the service, and assert that a customer is created and saved. Keeping these phases visually distinct makes failures easier to understand. Avoid placing many unrelated scenarios inside one test because a failure may not reveal which behavior was incorrect. Test names should describe the condition and expected result, such as shouldRejectRegistrationWhenEmailAlreadyExists. A consistent structure also makes code reviews easier because reviewers can quickly identify missing setup, unclear actions, and weak assertions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-3",
        "title": "Testing Business Rules and Service Decision Logic",
        "content": "Service unit tests should protect business decisions rather than merely confirm that methods execute without exceptions. Business rules may determine whether an order can be placed, whether a user can cancel a subscription, whether an account can withdraw funds, or whether a deployment request is eligible for approval. Each rule should have tests for valid conditions, invalid conditions, and important boundary values. For example, a service that permits a discount only when an order exceeds a minimum amount should be tested below the threshold, exactly at the threshold, and above it. Mock dependencies should return controlled data so the test focuses on the decision being made. Assertions should verify the resulting business outcome, not just that a repository method was called. If the business rule belongs in a domain object, test it there as well instead of duplicating all rule coverage in the service test. Good service tests provide confidence that application workflows apply business policies consistently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-4",
        "title": "Testing Input Validation and Boundary Conditions",
        "content": "Service methods often validate incoming values before performing expensive or irreversible operations. Tests should cover null inputs, blank strings, invalid identifiers, unsupported states, numeric limits, date boundaries, and combinations of invalid fields. For example, a transfer service may reject a zero amount, a negative amount, or a transfer that exceeds an account's permitted balance. Verify both the expected validation response and the absence of operations that must not happen after invalid input. Mockito verification can confirm that a payment gateway or repository was never called when validation fails. However, assertions should focus on the externally meaningful contract, such as the validation exception type or error code. Avoid creating a separate test for every meaningless permutation; prioritize boundaries that represent real business requirements. Validation tests should also distinguish between malformed input, valid input that violates a business rule, and unexpected dependency failures because these conditions may require different responses."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-5",
        "title": "Testing Service Collaboration with Repositories",
        "content": "A service frequently relies on repositories to retrieve and persist application data. Unit tests can configure repository responses to represent existing records, missing records, duplicate values, and persistence failures. For example, a registration service may call findByEmail before saving a new customer. One test can return an existing customer and verify that registration is rejected without saving another record. Another can return an empty result and verify that the new customer is saved with the expected values. These tests validate service decisions and repository collaboration, but they do not validate query correctness, ORM mappings, database constraints, or transaction behavior. Those require integration tests using the actual persistence technology. Avoid mocking the repository so broadly that impossible results are returned. The mock should follow the repository interface contract. When the service modifies an entity, assert the meaningful changed fields or capture the saved argument rather than relying only on a generic verify call."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-6",
        "title": "Testing Transactional Service Workflows",
        "content": "Transactional service methods often coordinate several data operations that must succeed or fail as one logical unit. A unit test can verify the service's decisions when one collaborator reports success or failure, but a plain Mockito test does not execute a real database transaction manager. For example, a transfer service may debit one account and credit another inside a transaction. A unit test can simulate a failure during the second operation and verify that the service propagates the expected exception or requests the defined compensation behavior. It cannot prove that the database actually rolls back the first update. Transaction propagation, isolation levels, rollback rules, and persistence-context behavior require framework integration tests. Be especially careful when testing annotations such as @Transactional because their behavior may depend on proxies and application context configuration. Keep service unit tests focused on orchestration and use integration tests to validate real transactional guarantees under the configured persistence technology."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-7",
        "title": "Testing Exception Translation and Error Handling",
        "content": "Services frequently translate low-level failures into application-specific exceptions or error results. A repository may throw a data-access exception, an external client may throw a timeout, or a messaging adapter may reject an operation. Unit tests should verify how the service exposes these failures to its callers. For example, a service may translate a missing repository record into CustomerNotFoundException while allowing an unexpected infrastructure failure to propagate through a documented error boundary. Use JUnit assertThrows to check the expected exception type and inspect important error codes or messages when those are part of the contract. Mockito can configure dependencies to throw specific exceptions so each failure path is deterministic. Also verify that success-only actions do not happen after a failure. Avoid catching every exception and returning a generic success-shaped response. Error-handling tests should ensure failures remain visible, meaningful, and consistent with the application's API or workflow contract."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-8",
        "title": "Testing Service Results, State Changes, and Returned DTOs",
        "content": "A service may return a domain object, a response DTO, a status value, or a summary assembled from multiple dependencies. Tests should verify the meaningful fields and state transitions in that result. For example, an account service may retrieve an account, apply a permitted change, save it, and return an AccountResponse containing the updated balance and status. Assertions should check the values that callers depend on rather than merely asserting that the returned object is not null. When mapping is part of the service responsibility, test important field conversions, default values, and omitted sensitive properties. If mapping is delegated to a dedicated mapper component, decide whether the service test should use the real mapper or a controlled mock based on the behavior being tested. Avoid brittle assertions on irrelevant formatting or generated metadata. Clear result assertions help catch incorrect calculations, stale values, missing fields, and accidental exposure of internal entity data."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-9",
        "title": "Testing Authorization and Security Context in Services",
        "content": "Service methods may enforce ownership, role permissions, tenant boundaries, or other access rules before allowing a business operation. Unit tests should cover authorized and unauthorized decisions using the application's security abstractions. For example, an account service may permit a user to view an account only when the account belongs to the authenticated principal or the principal has an administrator permission. Tests can provide a controlled security context or mock an authorization service, depending on the architecture. Verify that unauthorized requests are rejected before sensitive repository updates or external actions occur. Do not assume that a mocked authorization result proves the actual authentication configuration is correct. Token parsing, security filters, role mapping, and framework method-security configuration need integration tests. Avoid placing real credentials or production tokens in test fixtures. Security-focused service tests should explicitly document the protected operation, the required permission, and the expected denial behavior so future changes do not weaken access controls accidentally."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-10",
        "title": "Testing Service Events and Cross-Component Workflows",
        "content": "Services often coordinate workflows that involve more than one application component. A successful operation may update data, publish a domain event, schedule a background task, or request a notification. Unit tests should verify that these actions occur under the correct business conditions. For example, an order service should publish an OrderConfirmed event only after the order has passed validation and the required payment decision has succeeded. ArgumentCaptor can inspect the event payload and confirm that it contains the correct order identifier and relevant business details. Failure scenarios should verify that completion events are not published prematurely. If the workflow spans multiple independently failing systems, the service may rely on an outbox, saga, or compensation mechanism. Mockito tests can validate local orchestration decisions, but they cannot prove broker delivery, event ordering, or distributed consistency. Use integration tests to validate those infrastructure and messaging guarantees."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-11",
        "title": "Testing Asynchronous Service Methods and Background Tasks",
        "content": "Some service methods submit work to executors, return futures, invoke callbacks, or schedule background processing. Tests must distinguish between verifying that work was submitted and verifying that the work completed correctly. A deterministic approach is to inject an executor abstraction and capture the submitted task so the test can run it under controlled conditions. Callback-based services can be tested by capturing the callback and invoking it with controlled success and failure responses. Avoid relying on arbitrary Thread.sleep calls because timing assumptions create slow and flaky tests. For CompletableFuture-based workflows, assert the resulting completion value or exception using bounded waiting and predictable test inputs. If a framework proxy or scheduler triggers the asynchronous behavior, a framework-level integration test may be needed to validate the actual configuration. Keep unit tests focused on the service's scheduling decisions and result handling while using dedicated tests for real concurrency and thread-safety requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-12",
        "title": "Service Test Fixtures, Builders, and Reusable Test Data",
        "content": "Service tests often require complex domain objects with identifiers, statuses, dates, and related entities. Repeating long object construction in every test makes the suite difficult to read and maintain. Test fixture methods and builder objects can provide sensible defaults while allowing each test to override the fields relevant to its scenario. For example, a customer builder can create a valid customer by default and let a test change only the account status or email address. Reusable fixtures should not hide important behavior or create unexpected shared mutable state. Each test should receive independent objects when it modifies data. Avoid enormous global setup methods that configure every mock for every scenario because unused stubbings and hidden assumptions become difficult to diagnose. Keep scenario-specific values close to the test that uses them. Well-designed fixtures reduce duplication while making the business condition under test more visible to developers and reviewers."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-13",
        "title": "Service Test Coverage, Mutation Testing, and Quality Gaps",
        "content": "Code coverage measures which parts of the service implementation were executed by tests, but a high percentage does not automatically prove that business behavior is correct. A test may execute a branch without asserting the important result. Service testing should therefore combine coverage review with meaningful assertions and scenario analysis. Branch coverage can reveal untested decisions such as success versus failure, present versus missing data, or permitted versus denied access. Mutation testing provides another quality signal by introducing small code changes and checking whether tests detect them. If changing a comparison or removing a validation condition does not fail any test, the suite may lack an important assertion. Mutation testing tools add execution cost, so teams can apply them selectively to critical modules. Review uncovered paths based on business risk rather than chasing a percentage alone. A strong service test suite protects important decisions and failure behavior, not simply lines executed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-9-section-14",
        "title": "Service Unit Testing in Maven, Gradle, and CI Pipelines",
        "content": "Service unit tests should run consistently through the project's standard build lifecycle. Maven projects commonly execute them during the test phase using the configured test provider and Surefire plugin, while Gradle projects use the test task. A CI pipeline should check out the intended revision, configure a compatible JDK, resolve dependencies, compile source code, and execute the service tests. Test reports should be retained so developers can inspect failed test names, assertion messages, and stack traces. Keep unit tests independent from production credentials, shared databases, and external network services. If a test passes locally but fails in CI, investigate Java version differences, dependency versions, timezone assumptions, environment variables, test ordering, parallel execution, and shared mutable state. Critical service modules can have additional quality gates for coverage or mutation results. CI should make failures visible early and prevent a change with broken service behavior from progressing toward packaging or deployment."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Controller Testing",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-1",
        "title": "Controller Testing Scope and the HTTP Boundary",
        "content": "A controller is the entry point that receives an HTTP request, interprets route and request data, invokes application behavior, and constructs an HTTP response. Controller testing focuses on this web boundary rather than repeating all business-rule tests already covered at the service layer. A controller test should verify that the correct route accepts the request, input values are bound correctly, validation is applied, the intended application operation is invoked, and the response communicates the correct status and representation. For example, a customer endpoint may receive a POST request, validate the submitted JSON, call a customer application service, and return a 201 Created response. The test should verify this observable HTTP contract. It should not attempt to prove that the real database or external payment provider works. Those concerns belong in integration tests at the appropriate layer. Defining this boundary helps keep controller tests focused, readable, and valuable when the API evolves."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-2",
        "title": "Spring MVC Test Slice and Web Layer Configuration",
        "content": "In Spring Boot applications, the @WebMvcTest annotation can create a focused MVC test context containing selected web components instead of loading the complete application. This makes controller tests faster and reduces unrelated infrastructure setup. A test can target a specific controller class and provide its collaborators through @MockitoBean or the mock-bean mechanism supported by the project's Spring version. MVC configuration, argument resolvers, converters, validation integration, and relevant web advice can participate in the test slice. However, a slice test does not automatically prove that every production configuration class is loaded. If the application uses custom filters, security configuration, converters, or interceptors, confirm whether they are included or need explicit import. Use @SpringBootTest with MockMvc when the behavior depends on the complete application context. Choose the smallest test context that still exercises the web behavior being verified, and document any deliberately excluded configuration."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-3",
        "title": "MockMvc Request Construction and HTTP Method Testing",
        "content": "MockMvc allows tests to execute Spring MVC request handling without starting a real network server. Tests can construct requests using methods such as get, post, put, patch, and delete, then execute them through the configured MVC infrastructure. Request construction should represent realistic client behavior, including the path, query parameters, headers, content type, and request body. For example, a POST request may use contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON) and a JSON body representing a new resource. A PUT request may include a resource identifier and replacement fields, while a PATCH request may represent a partial update according to the API contract. Assertions should verify the expected HTTP status and relevant response details. Include negative cases such as unsupported methods, missing required parameters, and malformed request bodies. MockMvc exercises the Spring MVC dispatch pipeline, but it does not open a real TCP connection or validate a deployed reverse proxy, load balancer, or gateway."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-4",
        "title": "Route Mapping, Path Variables, and Query Parameters",
        "content": "Controller route mappings define how HTTP paths and methods reach controller handler methods. Tests should verify important mappings and confirm that path variables, query parameters, optional values, and default values are bound as intended. For example, GET /api/orders/{orderId} should pass the requested order identifier to the correct application operation. A search endpoint may accept status, page, size, and sort parameters, each with defined defaults and constraints. Tests should cover valid values, omitted optional parameters, malformed identifiers, unsupported enum values, and missing required parameters. They should also detect accidental route ambiguity where multiple handler methods could match the same request. Use MockMvc to verify externally observable routing behavior rather than testing framework annotations in isolation. When route names or API versions change, these tests provide early feedback about compatibility. Include both successful mapping and expected 404 or 405 behavior where those outcomes are part of the API contract."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-5",
        "title": "JSON Request Binding and Object Serialization",
        "content": "REST controllers commonly receive JSON request bodies and return JSON response representations. Controller tests should verify that incoming JSON is converted into the expected request object and that outgoing objects are serialized with the correct field names, value formats, and null-handling rules. MockMvc can submit JSON through content and assert response fields with jsonPath expressions. For example, a customer creation request may contain a name, email address, and contact preference, while the response may expose a generated identifier and status. Tests should cover missing fields, incorrect data types, invalid date formats, unknown properties when strict handling is configured, and nested object structures. Serialization behavior can be influenced by Jackson modules, naming strategies, custom serializers, and application configuration. A focused MVC test can validate the configured web conversion path, but a dedicated serialization test may be more appropriate for complex custom serializers. Never expose passwords, internal tokens, or confidential entity fields in response DTOs."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-6",
        "title": "Bean Validation and Controller Input Rejection",
        "content": "Controller request validation protects the HTTP boundary from invalid client data before application operations are invoked. Spring MVC commonly integrates Jakarta Bean Validation annotations such as @NotNull, @NotBlank, @Size, @Email, @Min, and @Valid. Tests should submit valid and invalid request bodies and verify the resulting HTTP status and error representation. For example, an invalid email address or blank required name may produce a 400 Bad Request response with field-level validation details. Also test constraints on path variables and query parameters when method validation is configured. A valuable assertion verifies that the controller does not call its application service when request validation fails. This prevents invalid requests from triggering side effects. Validation tests should distinguish structural input errors from business-rule conflicts that may use different status codes. Keep error payload assertions aligned with the public API contract, and avoid depending on unstable internal exception messages unless those messages are deliberately part of the contract."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-7",
        "title": "HTTP Status Codes, Headers, and Response Semantics",
        "content": "A controller response communicates more than a data object. Its status code, headers, and body together define the HTTP behavior clients depend on. Tests should verify successful responses such as 200 OK, 201 Created, 202 Accepted, and 204 No Content according to the operation's meaning. They should also verify appropriate client and server error responses, including 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, and 409 Conflict when relevant. For resource creation, a Location header may identify the newly created resource. For caching or content negotiation, headers such as ETag, Cache-Control, and Content-Type may be significant. A 204 response should not accidentally include a response body. Do not assert a successful status merely because the controller method returned normally; confirm that the status matches the API contract. Correct response semantics improve interoperability with browsers, mobile clients, API consumers, gateways, and automated integrations."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-8",
        "title": "Controller Exception Handling and Problem Responses",
        "content": "Controllers can rely on @ExceptionHandler methods or @ControllerAdvice and @RestControllerAdvice classes to translate application exceptions into HTTP responses. Tests should verify that known failures are represented consistently and that unexpected failures do not leak stack traces or internal implementation details. For example, a missing resource may map to 404 Not Found, a duplicate business operation may map to 409 Conflict, and invalid request data may map to 400 Bad Request. When an application uses RFC 9457 Problem Details, tests can assert fields such as type, title, status, detail, and instance where applicable. A focused MVC test must include the relevant advice class to exercise the intended exception translation. Also test that the response content type and error structure remain consistent across endpoints. Avoid asserting fragile stack-trace text or framework-generated messages. The goal is to protect the public error contract and ensure that clients receive enough information to respond correctly without exposing sensitive internals."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-9",
        "title": "Security Filters, Authentication, and Authorization Tests",
        "content": "Controller security tests verify how the web layer responds to authenticated and unauthenticated requests and whether access rules are applied to protected endpoints. With Spring Security test support, MockMvc can use request post-processors such as user, roles, and csrf to represent different security conditions. A protected endpoint should be tested for an anonymous request, an authenticated user with sufficient permissions, and an authenticated user without the required authority. For state-changing requests, verify CSRF behavior when CSRF protection is enabled. Tests should distinguish authentication failure, commonly represented by 401, from authorization denial, commonly represented by 403, according to the application's security configuration. Also verify that sensitive controller operations are not invoked when access is denied. Mock security identities do not validate real identity-provider integration, token signature verification, or production gateway configuration. Those require separate security integration and deployment tests."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-10",
        "title": "Content Negotiation, Media Types, and API Versioning",
        "content": "HTTP APIs may support different media types, response formats, or versioned routes. Controller tests should verify how the application handles Accept and Content-Type headers, including unsupported media types and incompatible response formats. For example, a JSON endpoint may produce application/json when the client accepts JSON and return an appropriate 406 Not Acceptable response when the requested representation is unavailable. A request with an unsupported body media type may receive 415 Unsupported Media Type. If the API supports versioned paths or headers, test that each supported version reaches the correct behavior and that deprecated versions remain compatible for the intended period. Do not assume that two endpoints are equivalent simply because they call the same service; response DTOs or field availability may differ by version. These tests protect client compatibility and make changes to converters, route mappings, and API version policies safer."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-11",
        "title": "Pagination, Sorting, Filtering, and Search Endpoint Tests",
        "content": "List and search controllers commonly accept pagination, sorting, and filtering parameters. Tests should verify that request parameters are translated into the expected application query and that response metadata correctly represents the returned page. Important cases include the first page, later pages, an empty result, the final partial page, invalid page sizes, unsupported sort fields, and multiple filter combinations. For example, a GET /api/orders request may accept page, size, status, and sort parameters. A controller test can capture the query object passed to the service and verify that the values were interpreted correctly. It should also check response fields such as content, totalElements, totalPages, number, and size when those fields are part of the API contract. Avoid testing database ordering or query performance with mocked service responses. Those require repository integration and performance tests. Keep pagination defaults and maximum limits explicit so clients cannot request unbounded result sets accidentally."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-12",
        "title": "Controller Interceptors, Filters, and Cross-Cutting Web Behavior",
        "content": "Web applications may apply cross-cutting behavior through servlet filters, Spring MVC interceptors, argument resolvers, and other request-processing components. Examples include correlation identifiers, request logging, locale selection, rate-limit metadata, and request context setup. Controller tests should include these components when their behavior affects the HTTP contract or controller execution. For example, a correlation ID filter may add a response header that clients use when reporting an incident. An interceptor may reject a request when a required tenant context is missing. Verify both the expected behavior and that the controller receives the correct context. A narrow @WebMvcTest may exclude custom configuration unless it is explicitly imported, so confirm the test context contains the intended component. Avoid testing implementation details that do not affect controller behavior in every controller test. Cross-cutting concerns can also have dedicated filter or interceptor tests, with a smaller number of MVC tests proving that they are correctly connected to the request pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-13",
        "title": "Controller Contract Tests and API Compatibility",
        "content": "A controller's public contract includes its route, accepted input, response status, headers, response schema, and documented error behavior. Contract-focused tests protect that agreement for consumers such as web applications, mobile applications, partner integrations, and other services. Tests should detect accidental changes such as renaming a JSON field, changing a required request property, removing a response header, or returning a different status code. Consumer-driven contract tools can complement controller tests when multiple independently deployed systems depend on the same API. Schema validation can also check whether responses conform to an OpenAPI definition. However, generated schema validation alone does not prove that the controller performs the correct business action. Combine contract assertions with focused behavior tests. When making a breaking API change, introduce an explicit versioning or migration plan rather than weakening tests to accept an unintended change. Strong controller contracts reduce integration surprises and make parallel team development safer."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-14",
        "title": "Controller Test Data, Fixtures, and Deterministic Responses",
        "content": "Controller tests need representative request and response objects, but large JSON strings and repeated object construction can make tests difficult to maintain. Use small fixture builders or reusable test resources for common request shapes while keeping scenario-specific values visible in each test. A response fixture should represent the public DTO, not expose persistence entities unnecessarily. Mock service responses should be deterministic and should not depend on current time, random identifiers, external services, or shared mutable state. If a response includes timestamps, inject a clock into the application layer where appropriate or assert a controlled range only when exact values cannot be fixed. Keep JSON test resources organized and review them when the API schema changes. Avoid using one enormous fixture for unrelated endpoint scenarios because hidden defaults can make failures difficult to understand. Good test data makes it clear which HTTP behavior is being exercised and allows developers to reproduce failures consistently on local machines and CI agents."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-10-section-15",
        "title": "Controller Test Failures, Diagnostics, and CI Quality Gates",
        "content": "When a controller test fails, first identify whether the failure occurred during test-context startup, route resolution, request binding, validation, security processing, controller execution, response serialization, or assertion evaluation. MockMvc output and application logs can help distinguish these layers. Common causes include an incorrect route, missing test bean, absent controller advice, invalid JSON, missing CSRF token, mismatched content type, and a security filter not loaded into the test context. In CI, ensure tests use the same supported JDK and dependency versions as local development and do not rely on environment-specific ports or credentials. Publish test reports and retain useful failure output so developers can inspect the exact request and response. Run focused controller tests during the normal verification phase and broader integration suites where appropriate. Quality gates should emphasize stable tests and meaningful API behavior rather than simply maximizing the number of assertions. Flaky controller tests often indicate shared state, timing assumptions, or inconsistent application configuration that should be corrected at its source."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Spring Boot Integration Testing",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-1",
        "title": "Integration Testing Objectives and the Spring Boot Application Boundary",
        "content": "Spring Boot integration testing verifies that multiple application components work together using the real framework configuration and infrastructure boundaries selected for the test. Unlike a service unit test that replaces collaborators with mocks, an integration test may load Spring beans, apply dependency injection, configure transaction management, and connect repositories to a test database. The objective is to discover failures that isolated tests cannot expose, such as incorrect bean wiring, incompatible configuration, ORM mapping errors, serialization problems, or security filter behavior. The scope should be deliberate: a focused repository integration test does not need to start the entire web application, while an end-to-end API workflow may need a running server and several infrastructure components. Define what each test proves and which dependencies are real or simulated. A useful suite combines fast unit tests, focused Spring integration tests, and a smaller number of broad application workflow tests. This layered approach provides meaningful confidence without making every code change wait for an unnecessarily expensive full-system test run."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-2",
        "title": "Spring ApplicationContext Loading and Bean Wiring Verification",
        "content": "The Spring ApplicationContext is responsible for creating application beans, resolving dependencies, applying configuration, and managing component lifecycles. Integration tests that load this context can reveal missing beans, ambiguous candidates, invalid configuration properties, circular dependencies, and incorrect conditional configuration. A context-loading test should represent a meaningful application configuration rather than blindly loading every possible module. Spring's test framework can cache compatible application contexts between test classes, reducing repeated startup cost. However, different active profiles, property overrides, imported configurations, or mock bean definitions may require separate cached contexts. Keep test configuration explicit so a passing test does not depend on accidental state left by another test. When a context fails to start, inspect the first meaningful root-cause exception rather than focusing only on the final Spring startup message. Context tests are valuable for configuration confidence, but they should not become a substitute for testing actual business workflows and infrastructure interactions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-3",
        "title": "Using @SpringBootTest for Full Application Integration Tests",
        "content": "@SpringBootTest is used when a test needs Spring Boot's application-level configuration and a broad set of application beans. It can discover the main application configuration, create the test ApplicationContext, and allow tests to inject services, repositories, or HTTP testing clients. The webEnvironment attribute controls how the web environment is created. MOCK configures a mock web environment where supported, RANDOM_PORT starts a real embedded server on an available port, DEFINED_PORT uses a configured port, and NONE avoids creating a web environment. Choose the smallest mode that proves the intended behavior. A full context test is appropriate for workflows involving several configured components, but it is slower than a focused test slice. Avoid adding @SpringBootTest to every test class because unnecessary context startup increases build time and can make failures harder to isolate. Keep external dependencies controlled so a full application context does not accidentally connect to production services."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-4",
        "title": "Selecting Test Slices with @WebMvcTest and @DataJpaTest",
        "content": "Spring Boot test slices load only a selected part of the application to make integration tests more focused. @WebMvcTest is designed for MVC controller-layer testing and typically configures MVC infrastructure, request mapping, conversion, validation, and relevant web components without loading the entire application. Collaborating services are commonly supplied as test doubles. @DataJpaTest focuses on JPA repositories and persistence configuration, making it useful for entity mappings, repository queries, and database behavior. Slice tests reduce startup cost and help identify which application layer is failing. They also have boundaries: a web slice does not prove that the real production database works, and a JPA slice does not validate the entire HTTP security and controller workflow. If a required component is excluded from the slice, import it intentionally or choose a broader test. Understand each annotation's auto-configuration behavior and avoid adding unrelated configuration that turns a focused test into an expensive full-context test."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-5",
        "title": "Testing HTTP Controllers with MockMvc and Request Processing",
        "content": "MockMvc allows tests to exercise Spring MVC request processing without requiring a real network connection to an embedded server. It can submit requests with HTTP methods, paths, query parameters, headers, content types, and request bodies, then inspect status codes, response headers, and serialized content. This makes it useful for verifying controller mappings, validation behavior, exception handling, and response contracts. A test can send malformed JSON and confirm that the application returns the expected client error, or submit a valid request and inspect the response DTO. When the test uses the Spring MVC test context, relevant filters and application configuration can be included according to the setup. MockMvc does not prove real socket behavior, reverse-proxy configuration, TLS termination, or deployment networking. Use a real embedded server test when those boundaries matter. Keep assertions focused on the API contract, including meaningful status codes, stable response fields, and error formats rather than incidental implementation details."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-6",
        "title": "Testing Real HTTP Server Behavior with Random Port",
        "content": "A test using @SpringBootTest with webEnvironment set to RANDOM_PORT starts the embedded web server on an available port. This allows an HTTP client such as TestRestTemplate or WebTestClient to send requests through the actual server socket and exercise request handling beyond the mock MVC boundary. It is useful for validating server startup, HTTP request processing, serialization, filters, and interactions between controllers and application services. RANDOM_PORT avoids fixed-port conflicts when multiple builds or test processes run concurrently. The test should obtain the assigned port from the test environment rather than hardcoding a development port. Real-server tests are slower and may require more careful cleanup of data and background resources. They still do not reproduce every production networking layer, such as ingress controllers, load balancers, DNS, or TLS infrastructure. Use them for workflows where the real embedded server behavior is part of the requirement."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-7",
        "title": "Testing REST API Contracts, Serialization, and Error Responses",
        "content": "REST integration tests should verify the API contract that clients actually consume. This includes request methods, URL paths, required headers, accepted content types, validation rules, response status codes, response bodies, and error representations. Serialization tests should check that Java objects are converted to the expected JSON field names, data types, date formats, and nested structures. Deserialization tests should confirm that incoming JSON is interpreted correctly and invalid values are rejected consistently. Error scenarios deserve equal attention: missing resources, invalid input, unauthorized requests, and unexpected server failures should produce suitable status codes and predictable response structures. Avoid asserting every field when only a small stable contract matters, but do assert fields whose omission would break clients. If the API uses a shared error response format, test representative exceptions across the configured exception-handling mechanism. These tests protect compatibility between the backend and consumers such as browser applications, mobile clients, automation scripts, and other services."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-8",
        "title": "Database Integration Testing with Realistic Test Databases",
        "content": "Database integration tests validate the interaction between Spring Data repositories, ORM mappings, SQL queries, constraints, and the database engine. An in-memory database can provide fast feedback for basic persistence behavior, but it may differ from the production database in SQL syntax, data types, locking, indexes, and constraint behavior. For important production workflows, use the same database engine or a compatible version in an isolated test environment. Tests should verify entity persistence, relationships, generated identifiers, unique constraints, query methods, pagination, sorting, and transaction behavior. Avoid relying only on mocked repository interfaces because mocks cannot detect invalid JPQL, incorrect mappings, or database constraint failures. Ensure each test starts with known data and does not depend on records created by another test. Keep connection settings and credentials isolated from production. A realistic test database gives stronger confidence that the application persistence layer behaves correctly under the database technology it will actually use."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-9",
        "title": "Containerized Infrastructure Testing with Testcontainers",
        "content": "Testcontainers provides disposable infrastructure dependencies for automated tests by launching containers through a compatible container runtime. It is commonly used to run a real PostgreSQL, MySQL, Redis, Kafka, or other supported service during a test suite. This allows integration tests to use realistic infrastructure without requiring a permanently shared test server. The test obtains the container's mapped connection details and supplies them to the Spring application, often through dynamic properties or supported service connection integration. Containers should use explicit image versions so a test does not silently change behavior when an upstream latest tag is updated. Startup readiness checks, resource cleanup, runtime availability, and CI permissions must be considered. Reusable containers may improve speed in controlled environments, but they can introduce shared-state risks if tests are not isolated. Testcontainers is especially useful when an in-memory substitute cannot reproduce production database or messaging behavior. It does not eliminate the need to manage test data and container lifecycle carefully."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-10",
        "title": "Repository Query and ORM Mapping Integration Tests",
        "content": "Repository integration tests verify that persistence operations work with the configured ORM provider and database. They should cover custom query methods, derived query names, JPQL or native SQL, joins, relationship loading, pagination, sorting, and entity lifecycle behavior. A query that compiles successfully may still return incorrect results because of join conditions, null handling, duplicate rows, or unexpected lazy loading. Prepare a small dataset that clearly distinguishes matching and non-matching records, execute the repository operation, and assert the actual returned data. Flush and clear the persistence context when necessary so the test does not receive an entity already held in the first-level cache instead of reading it back from the database. Check important constraints and relationships using realistic records. Avoid testing only generated CRUD methods when the application relies on complex custom queries. Repository tests should reveal problems in the persistence mapping and query behavior that cannot be discovered by mocking the repository interface."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-11",
        "title": "Transaction Boundaries, Rollback, and Test Data Isolation",
        "content": "Spring transaction management affects when database changes become visible and whether failed operations are rolled back. Integration tests should validate transaction boundaries when the application depends on atomic operations, propagation behavior, isolation, or rollback rules. A test method annotated with @Transactional may be rolled back after completion in common Spring test configurations, which helps prevent persistent test data. However, this behavior can hide problems when the application code commits in a separate transaction, uses asynchronous work, or communicates with another system. Some database operations require explicit flushes before assertions can observe constraint violations. Test cleanup should not depend on rollback alone if the test starts external processes or writes to resources outside the transaction. Verify that failure paths do not leave partial database updates when atomicity is required. Also ensure that parallel tests do not compete for the same unique records or shared mutable rows. Correct transaction tests help establish the real consistency guarantees of the application."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-12",
        "title": "Security Integration Testing with Authentication and Authorization",
        "content": "Security integration tests verify that authentication and authorization rules are applied through the configured Spring Security chain. They should cover anonymous requests, valid authenticated requests, insufficient permissions, role-based access, ownership checks, and protected administrative operations. Depending on the application, tests may use Spring Security test support to create an authenticated test principal or submit a real token through the configured authentication mechanism. A mocked principal can validate controller authorization decisions but does not prove that JWT signature verification, token expiry, issuer checks, or filter configuration work correctly. Include tests for invalid credentials and expired or malformed tokens when token processing is part of the application's security boundary. Confirm that sensitive endpoints do not return protected data to unauthorized callers. Avoid embedding production secrets or real user tokens in fixtures. Security tests should reflect the actual authorization policy and ensure that future controller or configuration changes do not accidentally expose restricted operations."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-13",
        "title": "Messaging Integration Tests for Kafka, RabbitMQ, and Event Workflows",
        "content": "Applications that publish or consume messages need tests beyond checking that a publisher method was called. Messaging integration tests can start a real broker through Testcontainers and verify serialization, topic or queue configuration, consumer processing, acknowledgment behavior, and error handling. A producer test should publish an event and confirm that the intended message reaches the configured destination with the expected key, headers, and payload. A consumer test should send a message and verify the application workflow that processes it. Account for asynchronous delivery by waiting for a defined condition with a bounded timeout rather than using arbitrary sleep durations. Isolate topics, queues, consumer groups, and test data so parallel runs do not interfere with one another. Test retry and dead-letter behavior where those mechanisms are part of the application contract. A mocked broker client can be useful in unit tests, but only a real broker integration test can expose incompatible broker settings, serialization problems, or protocol-level failures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-14",
        "title": "External HTTP Service Integration and WireMock Stubbing",
        "content": "Spring Boot services frequently call external HTTP APIs for payments, identity, inventory, notifications, or cloud operations. Integration tests should verify that the configured HTTP client, request serialization, headers, authentication handling, timeout settings, and response mapping work together. WireMock or a similar controlled HTTP server can simulate the external service while the application's real HTTP client configuration remains active. Define deterministic responses for success, client errors, server errors, malformed payloads, slow responses, and connection failures. Verify important outgoing request details when the external contract requires them. Keep the mock server isolated and assign ports dynamically to avoid conflicts. A WireMock test does not prove that the real provider is available or that its current API behavior matches the stub. Contract tests and controlled sandbox tests may provide additional assurance. Never make ordinary CI integration tests depend on live payment or production endpoints, because network instability and real side effects make builds unreliable and unsafe."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-15",
        "title": "Profiles, Properties, and Environment-Specific Configuration Testing",
        "content": "Spring Boot applications commonly use profiles and externalized properties to select configuration for local development, testing, staging, and production. Integration tests should confirm that the intended test profile activates the correct beans, database settings, feature flags, and endpoint configuration. @ActiveProfiles can select a profile for a test class, while test property overrides can supply controlled values for a specific scenario. Test properties should not accidentally point to production databases, real message brokers, or live external APIs. Conditional bean creation should also be checked when different properties determine whether an integration is enabled. A common defect occurs when a required property exists locally but is missing from the deployment environment. Configuration-focused tests can detect missing values and incorrect defaults early. Keep secrets outside committed test files and use safe placeholders or dedicated secret injection in controlled environments. The goal is to verify that the application selects a predictable test configuration without confusing test-only behavior with production configuration."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-16",
        "title": "Database Migration Integration Tests with Flyway or Liquibase",
        "content": "Database migration tools such as Flyway and Liquibase evolve the database schema as application versions change. Integration tests should verify that migrations execute successfully against the intended database engine and create the schema expected by the application. A useful migration test starts with an empty database, applies the migration history, and checks important tables, columns, indexes, constraints, and reference data. It can also reveal invalid SQL, incorrect migration ordering, unsupported database features, and conflicts between migration scripts. Avoid relying only on a developer database that already contains manually created objects, because this can hide missing migrations. Test upgrades from supported earlier schema versions when the release process requires them. Migration scripts should be treated as versioned deployment artifacts, and tests should not casually modify historical migrations that have already been applied in production. Running migrations in a disposable database through Testcontainers provides realistic feedback before a new application version is deployed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-17",
        "title": "Test Lifecycle, Context Caching, and Reliable Resource Cleanup",
        "content": "Spring integration tests create resources that may outlive a single test method, including application contexts, embedded servers, containers, broker connections, and background executors. Understand the lifecycle of each resource and make cleanup explicit where automatic management is not sufficient. Spring can cache compatible application contexts to improve performance, but context changes and dirty-state annotations may cause additional context creation. Avoid marking every class as dirty because this can dramatically increase execution time. Test data should be isolated through transactions, unique identifiers, database cleanup, or disposable infrastructure. Containers and mock servers should stop reliably after the test scope ends. Background threads and scheduled tasks must not continue changing data after assertions complete. When tests fail only after other tests have run, investigate shared static state, cached beans, global configuration, and resource leakage. A reliable lifecycle strategy makes the suite repeatable locally and in CI, reduces intermittent failures, and prevents one test from influencing the result of another."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-18",
        "title": "Testing Asynchronous Processing and Eventual Consistency",
        "content": "Asynchronous workflows introduce timing differences between accepting a request and completing its resulting work. An HTTP request may enqueue a message, after which a consumer updates a database or calls another service. Integration tests should wait for a business condition that proves the workflow completed, rather than assuming a fixed delay is sufficient. Use bounded polling, test synchronization utilities, or observable completion signals. Define a clear timeout so a missing event becomes a useful test failure instead of an indefinitely hanging build. When the system is eventually consistent, assertions should account for the documented delay without accepting incorrect final states. Verify duplicate message handling, retry behavior, idempotency, and failure recovery when these are important requirements. A test should also confirm that background processing does not execute twice because of accidental test setup. Avoid unbounded polling and excessive sleep calls. These tests help expose race conditions, consumer configuration mistakes, and incomplete workflows that ordinary synchronous controller tests cannot reveal."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-11-section-19",
        "title": "Integration Test Reporting, Diagnostics, and CI Quality Gates",
        "content": "Integration test reports should provide enough information to identify whether a failure came from application logic, framework configuration, database behavior, container startup, network communication, or test infrastructure. Maven Surefire and Failsafe reports and Gradle test reports can expose failed test names, assertion messages, and stack traces. Preserve relevant logs and diagnostic output when a CI job fails. For database failures, inspect SQL exceptions, constraint names, and generated queries. For context startup failures, locate the earliest meaningful bean creation or configuration error. For container failures, inspect image availability, runtime permissions, startup logs, and readiness checks. Avoid logging passwords, access tokens, personal information, or sensitive request bodies. Test names should describe the workflow and scenario so reports remain understandable when many suites execute. A useful failure report should help a developer reproduce the problem locally and distinguish a genuine application regression from a temporary infrastructure issue.\n\nPRODUCTION CI PIPELINE STRATEGY\nA production-ready integration test strategy balances confidence, execution time, isolation, and maintenance cost. Run fast unit tests early, focused Spring slice tests next, and broader database, messaging, and HTTP integration tests in appropriate pipeline stages. Use disposable infrastructure where practical and pin container image versions to keep results reproducible. Separate tests that require special infrastructure from ordinary application tests, but make their execution status visible so skipped coverage is not mistaken for a successful validation. CI agents should provide the required container runtime, compatible JDK, sufficient memory, and network access to approved dependency registries. Publish test reports even when a test stage fails, and prevent packaging or deployment when required quality gates fail. Track slow tests, context startup time, flaky failures, and infrastructure-related failures over time. A dependable suite should run from a clean checkout, avoid production resources, produce actionable diagnostics, and validate the application's critical integration contracts before release."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Testcontainers",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-1",
        "title": "Testcontainers Architecture and the Role of Disposable Infrastructure",
        "content": "Testcontainers is a testing library that provisions real infrastructure dependencies inside containers for automated tests. Instead of replacing every database, broker, or cache with an in-memory substitute, a test can start an actual service image and communicate with it through mapped ports. The library coordinates container creation, startup, readiness checks, connection details, and cleanup through a compatible container runtime such as Docker or another supported environment. A test typically declares the required container, starts it before the application component needs it, reads its dynamically mapped endpoint, and stops it when the test lifecycle ends. This approach improves confidence in database dialects, broker protocols, authentication settings, and infrastructure-specific behavior. Testcontainers does not make a test a production deployment replica: it usually runs a focused dependency in isolation rather than the complete production platform. Select containers according to the integration boundary being tested, and keep application business assertions separate from container-management concerns."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-2",
        "title": "Container Runtime Requirements and Local Development Setup",
        "content": "Testcontainers requires access to a container runtime capable of pulling images, creating containers, exposing ports, and managing their lifecycle. On a developer workstation, this is commonly provided by Docker Desktop or a compatible Docker Engine installation. The runtime must be running, and the current user or process must have permission to access its API. In Linux environments, socket permissions and daemon availability are frequent causes of startup failures. In CI, the build agent must provide a supported runtime and network access to the required image registry. Developers should verify runtime availability before investigating application test code. Runtime configuration can vary between local machines, remote Docker hosts, and CI agents, so avoid hardcoding a local socket path or assuming that every environment uses the same endpoint. Document the supported runtime setup, required permissions, and any proxy or registry configuration. A predictable runtime environment prevents infrastructure setup problems from being confused with application defects."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-3",
        "title": "Declaring Containers and Managing Their Lifecycle",
        "content": "A Testcontainers test declares the infrastructure image it needs and configures the container before startup. Configuration may include environment variables, exposed ports, startup commands, mounted resources, network aliases, and readiness conditions. Containers should start before the application attempts to connect and should remain available for the complete test scope. The lifecycle can be managed through JUnit 5 integration, class-level container declarations, or explicit programmatic startup and shutdown. Class-scoped containers can reduce startup time when several tests safely share one service instance, while method-scoped containers provide stronger isolation at a higher execution cost. Choose lifecycle boundaries according to state sharing and cleanup requirements. Avoid starting containers from arbitrary test methods without a clear ownership model, because failed tests may leave resources running. Make lifecycle behavior explicit and ensure that container cleanup occurs even when assertions fail. Reliable lifecycle management keeps test execution repeatable and prevents resource leaks."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-4",
        "title": "Image Selection, Version Pinning, and Container Configuration",
        "content": "The container image determines the software version and behavior exercised by an integration test. Pin images to an explicit version or digest instead of using a floating latest tag, because an upstream image update can change defaults, authentication requirements, startup behavior, or database compatibility without any change to the application repository. Select versions that match the production compatibility target whenever the test is intended to validate production integration. Configure required environment variables, initialization scripts, database names, users, and passwords before starting the container. Keep non-secret test credentials limited to disposable test environments and never reuse production secrets. If an image requires an initialization command or custom configuration file, include that setup in a reproducible test resource. Review image licensing, provenance, vulnerability status, and registry availability as part of the development process. A pinned and documented image makes local and CI test results more consistent and easier to reproduce."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-5",
        "title": "Dynamic Port Mapping and Container Connection Details",
        "content": "Testcontainers commonly maps a container's internal service port to an available host port. This avoids collisions when multiple developers or CI jobs run tests at the same time. The application must use the mapped host and port returned by the running container rather than assuming that the internal container port is directly available on the host. For example, a database may listen on port 5432 inside its container while the runtime maps it to a different host port. Connection URLs should therefore be assembled from the container's runtime connection information. Hardcoded localhost ports can cause intermittent failures or accidentally connect a test to a developer's unrelated service. When configuring Spring Boot properties, supply the resolved values after the container starts. Also consider whether the test process and container runtime are on the same host or separated by a remote Docker environment. Correct endpoint discovery is essential for reliable integration tests across laptops and CI agents."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-6",
        "title": "Container Networking, Aliases, and Multi-Service Test Environments",
        "content": "Some integration scenarios require several containers to communicate, such as an application dependency connecting to a database and a message broker. Testcontainers supports creating an isolated network and attaching containers with network aliases. Containers on that network can communicate using their configured aliases and internal service ports, while the test process may use mapped host ports to reach them from outside. This distinction matters because a hostname that works from the test JVM may not resolve inside another container. Define the network before starting dependent containers and ensure startup order follows actual dependency requirements. For example, a test application container may need the database container to become ready before its own startup completes. Avoid relying on a developer's shared Docker network or fixed container names. Isolated networks reduce accidental cross-test communication and make multi-service test environments more reproducible. Network design should mirror only the communication paths relevant to the behavior under test."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-7",
        "title": "Readiness Checks, Wait Strategies, and Startup Reliability",
        "content": "A container being created does not mean that its service is ready to accept requests. Databases may still be applying initialization scripts, brokers may still be forming a cluster, and HTTP services may need additional startup time. Testcontainers wait strategies define how the test determines that a dependency is ready. Depending on the image, readiness can be detected through a listening port, a log message, an HTTP response, or a custom health condition. Choose a signal that represents actual service readiness rather than simply checking that the container process exists. Startup timeouts should be long enough for realistic CI environments but bounded so a broken dependency does not hang the build indefinitely. When a readiness check fails, inspect container logs and the selected condition before increasing the timeout blindly. Custom wait strategies may be needed for services with complex startup sequences. Reliable readiness checks reduce flaky tests caused by race conditions between container startup and application connection attempts."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-8",
        "title": "Integrating Testcontainers with JUnit 5 Test Lifecycle",
        "content": "Testcontainers provides integration with JUnit 5 so containers can be managed alongside test class and method execution. The JUnit Jupiter extension supports lifecycle patterns in which declared containers are started and stopped according to their configured scope. A static container field is commonly used when a class shares one container across its test methods, while an instance-level container can be associated with an individual test instance lifecycle. The correct choice depends on whether tests can safely share database records, broker state, or other mutable information. Sharing a container does not automatically isolate the data stored inside it. Tests must still clean up or uniquely identify their records. Ensure that the JUnit extension is configured consistently with the project's test framework version and that the necessary Testcontainers modules are included. Avoid mixing manual lifecycle management with extension-managed startup unless the ownership and shutdown responsibilities are clearly defined."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-9",
        "title": "Spring Boot Integration Using Dynamic Properties and Service Connections",
        "content": "Spring Boot applications need connection properties before they can create clients, data sources, or other infrastructure beans. Testcontainers can supply runtime connection information to the Spring test context through dynamic property registration or supported service connection integration. Dynamic properties are useful when the test needs to map container-provided hostnames, ports, usernames, passwords, or database names into the application's configuration. Supported Spring Boot versions also provide service connection mechanisms that can reduce manual property wiring for recognized container types. Confirm compatibility between the Spring Boot, Testcontainers, and JUnit versions used by the project. A common failure occurs when the Spring context starts before the container is ready or when the application reads a fixed property instead of the dynamically supplied value. Keep test configuration focused and avoid maintaining two competing sources of connection details. Verify that the application bean actually connects to the intended container rather than a local or shared development service."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-10",
        "title": "Database Testing with PostgreSQL, MySQL, and Other Real Engines",
        "content": "Testcontainers can launch real database engines so integration tests exercise the same database family used by the application. This is important because in-memory databases may differ in SQL syntax, data types, locking behavior, indexing, constraint enforcement, and transaction semantics. A PostgreSQL container, for example, allows repository tests to execute against PostgreSQL rather than assuming that another database behaves identically. Configure the database image, credentials, database name, and initialization scripts before connecting Spring Data or JDBC components. Tests should cover repository queries, entity relationships, unique constraints, pagination, generated identifiers, and transaction behavior where relevant. Use controlled test data and ensure that each test can run independently. Database containers are particularly useful for validating migration scripts and native SQL. They do not replace production-scale performance testing or high-availability testing, but they provide much stronger compatibility evidence than mocks or an unrelated in-memory database."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-11",
        "title": "Testing Kafka, RabbitMQ, Redis, and Messaging Dependencies",
        "content": "Modern services often depend on messaging systems and caches in addition to relational databases. Testcontainers can start supported Kafka, RabbitMQ, Redis, and other infrastructure images so application integration tests use real protocols and server behavior. A Kafka test can verify producer configuration, serialization, topic interaction, and consumer processing. A RabbitMQ test can validate exchanges, queues, routing keys, and message acknowledgment behavior. Redis tests can verify cache serialization, expiration, and data access against a real Redis server. Configure unique topics, queue names, keys, or namespaces so parallel test runs do not interfere. Asynchronous processing requires bounded waiting for an observable outcome instead of arbitrary Thread.sleep calls. A passing container test demonstrates compatibility with the tested image and configuration, not with every possible production cluster topology. Use additional deployment or resilience tests when replication, failover, partitioning, or cluster-wide behavior is part of the requirement."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-12",
        "title": "Initialization Scripts, Seed Data, and Repeatable Test Fixtures",
        "content": "Infrastructure containers often require a known starting state before application tests execute. Testcontainers supports initialization mechanisms such as database scripts, mounted configuration files, and application-specific setup operations. Use these mechanisms to create the minimum schema, reference records, topics, or other resources required by the scenario. Keep initialization scripts version-controlled and deterministic so a clean checkout produces the same environment. Avoid large production database dumps because they increase startup time and may contain sensitive information. Test fixtures should be small enough to understand and should not depend on execution order. If several tests share one container, use unique identifiers or explicit cleanup to prevent one scenario from affecting another. Validate that initialization completes before the test starts its assertions. When database migrations are owned by Flyway or Liquibase, prefer exercising those migrations rather than maintaining a separate schema definition that can drift away from the real application."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-13",
        "title": "State Isolation, Cleanup, and Parallel Test Execution",
        "content": "A shared container can improve test performance, but the data and state inside it may cause tests to influence each other. Isolation can be achieved through unique database schemas, separate databases, transaction rollback where appropriate, unique queue or topic names, and explicit deletion of records created by a test. Do not assume that stopping and restarting an application context automatically clears the container's data. Parallel execution adds further risks when two tests modify the same account, cache key, table row, or message destination. Use independent test identifiers and avoid global mutable fixtures. If the infrastructure cannot safely support parallel tests, configure a controlled execution strategy rather than allowing unpredictable collisions. Cleanup should be reliable even when an assertion fails. Review container reuse carefully: reusing a container across separate test runs can save startup time but may preserve stale state. Strong isolation makes tests deterministic and allows CI workers to execute more scenarios concurrently without creating false failures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-14",
        "title": "Testing External Service Containers and Multi-Container Dependencies",
        "content": "Some applications depend on services that are difficult to reproduce with mocks alone, including local cloud emulators, search engines, identity providers, or specialized HTTP services. A compatible container image can provide a controlled instance for integration tests. Multi-container scenarios should declare explicit dependencies, networks, startup conditions, and endpoint configuration. For example, an application container may need both a database and an identity provider before it can complete startup. Keep each dependency version pinned and configure only the features required by the test. If a service image requires licensing, special startup flags, or initialization credentials, document those requirements and keep them outside production secrets. Containerized substitutes may not reproduce every feature or limitation of a managed cloud service. Verify the fidelity of the substitute against the behavior being tested and use provider sandbox or contract testing where necessary. This approach gives realistic local integration coverage while keeping tests independent from live external systems."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-15",
        "title": "Container Logs, Diagnostics, and Troubleshooting Startup Failures",
        "content": "When a Testcontainers test fails, first determine whether the failure occurred during image pulling, container creation, startup, readiness detection, endpoint discovery, or application communication. Inspect the container logs for missing environment variables, permission errors, invalid configuration, database initialization failures, or port binding problems. Verify that the container runtime is running and that the test process can access it. Check whether the selected image tag exists and whether the registry is reachable from the current network. A readiness timeout may indicate that the service is genuinely unhealthy rather than simply slow. Inspect mapped ports and host information before assuming that the application is connecting to the correct endpoint. In CI, also check container runtime permissions, resource limits, proxy configuration, and image pull credentials. Preserve useful logs when a test fails, but do not expose passwords, tokens, or sensitive application data. Categorizing the failure by lifecycle stage prevents random changes to application code when the real problem is infrastructure setup."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-16",
        "title": "Performance Optimization, Image Caching, and Resource Management",
        "content": "Container startup and image download time can become a significant part of a large integration test suite. Optimize the suite by using focused test scopes, reusing compatible containers within a controlled class or suite, and avoiding unnecessary infrastructure dependencies. Pin images so CI caching can work reliably, and use approved registry mirrors or preloaded images where organizational policies allow. Do not share mutable service state merely to improve speed. Measure startup time, image pull time, database initialization time, and test execution time separately so the real bottleneck is visible. Configure resource limits appropriately for CI workers, especially when several database and broker containers start concurrently. Clean up unused containers, volumes, and networks so repeated runs do not exhaust disk space or memory. Parallel execution can reduce elapsed time, but only after state isolation is reliable. Performance improvements should preserve deterministic behavior and should never depend on stale data from previous test runs."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-12-section-17",
        "title": "Secure and Production-Ready Testcontainers Strategy in CI/CD",
        "content": "A production-ready Testcontainers strategy treats test infrastructure as controlled and disposable rather than as a shared production-like environment. CI agents need a supported container runtime, sufficient CPU and memory, access to approved image registries, and permissions to create and remove containers. Use trusted, pinned images and scan them according to the organization's security process. Keep credentials limited to test-only values and never mount production secrets or sensitive host directories into test containers. Publish test reports and relevant sanitized container logs so failed runs can be investigated. Make infrastructure-dependent test stages visible in the pipeline and do not treat skipped tests as successful coverage. Apply timeouts and cleanup policies so failed jobs do not leave resources running. Monitor image pull failures, startup duration, flaky tests, and resource consumption over time. A reliable strategy should work from a clean checkout, isolate concurrent builds, protect secrets, and validate real infrastructure compatibility before the application advances toward deployment."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Code Coverage",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-13-section-1",
        "title": "Code Coverage — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Code Coverage — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Code Coverage** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nDefine the Kubernetes concept represented by this section and explain the problem it solves. Describe where it fits in a cluster and application lifecycle. Identify the relevant API resource, controller, node component, or external integration. Explain technical terms in beginner-friendly language and distinguish Kubernetes orchestration from the responsibility of the application running inside a container.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes. The kubelet on each worker node works with the container runtime to ensure assigned workloads run as specified.\n\nFor **Code Coverage — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals**, identify the API resource, controller, node component, or external integration responsible for the behavior. Explain what state is submitted, what reconciliation takes place, and how the result can be observed. Do not assume that a successful kubectl apply means the application is healthy. API acceptance, scheduling, container startup, readiness, and application availability are separate conditions.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the current Kubernetes context with kubectl config current-context.\n2. Select and verify the correct namespace.\n3. Inspect the relevant resources before changing anything.\n4. Review the manifest fields, image reference, labels, selectors, resources, and permissions.\n5. Apply the required configuration using a reviewed manifest or approved deployment process.\n6. Observe controller reconciliation and Pod scheduling.\n7. Inspect Pod conditions, container state, events, and logs.\n8. Verify networking, storage, and application readiness where required.\n9. Record the manifest revision, image digest, and resulting workload state.\n\nUse namespaces and explicit resource names to avoid changing the wrong environment. Before deleting or replacing resources, determine whether they own persistent data or production traffic.\n\n## 4. Practical Kubernetes Commands\n\nThe following commands provide relevant inspection and operational examples. Replace placeholder resource names and namespaces with actual values.\n\n`\bash id=\"k8s501\"\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get namespaces\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl get services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n`\n\nCommands that modify resources should be executed only after checking the active context and reviewing the target manifest. Avoid copying production credentials or secret values into terminal history.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes Workload Example\n\nThe following Deployment demonstrates a basic stateless application workload:\n\n`yaml id=\"k8s502\"\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: application\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.0\n          ports:\n            - name: http\n              containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"250m\"\n              memory: \"256Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 30\n            periodSeconds: 10\n`\n\nThe Deployment declares three desired replicas. Its controller creates and maintains the Pods. Resource requests help the scheduler select suitable nodes, while limits constrain container consumption. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic. The example must be adapted to the actual application health endpoints and resource profile.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment application deployed to a Kubernetes cluster. The development team stores its source code and Kubernetes manifests in version control. A CI pipeline builds and tests the application, scans the image, and publishes an immutable image digest to a registry.\n\nA delivery process updates the Deployment manifest to reference the approved image. Kubernetes accepts the updated desired state and begins a rolling update. The scheduler selects nodes with sufficient available resources. Kubelets start the containers, and readiness probes determine when the new Pods can receive traffic.\n\nThe Service routes requests to ready endpoints. Monitoring records application latency, error rates, Pod restarts, CPU usage, memory usage, and node health. If the new version fails readiness or application validation, the delivery process stops or rolls back according to the deployment policy.\n\nThe team records the source commit, image digest, manifest revision, deployment time, and validation result. This makes it possible to identify exactly which application version is running during an incident.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Applying manifests to the wrong Kubernetes context can change an unintended cluster.\n- Incorrect labels or selectors can leave Services without matching Pod endpoints.\n- Missing resource requests can make scheduling unpredictable under resource pressure.\n- Excessively low memory limits can cause containers to be terminated with OOMKilled.\n- Incorrect readiness probes can prevent healthy applications from receiving traffic.\n- Incorrect liveness probes can repeatedly restart an application that is still recovering.\n- Using mutable image tags weakens deployment traceability.\n- Broad RBAC permissions increase the impact of compromised identities.\n- Deleting PersistentVolumeClaims without understanding reclaim behavior can cause data loss.\n- Assuming Pod creation means application readiness can cause outages.\n- Ignoring namespace boundaries can lead to incorrect resource inspection or modification.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by confirming the active context, namespace, resource name, and timestamp of the failure. Inspect resource state using kubectl get and kubectl describe. Review events for scheduling failures, image-pull errors, mount failures, probe failures, and node pressure.\n\nFor a Pending Pod, inspect scheduling events, node capacity, resource requests, taints, tolerations, and affinity. For ImagePullBackOff, verify the image reference, registry availability, and image-pull credentials. For CrashLoopBackOff, inspect current and previous container logs, exit codes, commands, configuration, and resource limits.\n\nFor service connectivity problems, inspect Service selectors, EndpointSlices, DNS, network policies, and the cluster network implementation. For storage failures, inspect PVC binding, PV state, CSI components, access modes, and mount events.\n\nApply the smallest safe correction. Preserve evidence before deleting Pods or changing shared resources. Verify the recovery through resource conditions and an actual application request.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC for human users and service accounts. Avoid granting cluster-admin access to application workloads. Protect Secrets through encryption at rest, restrictive access, controlled rotation, and secure external secret integration where required.\n\nUse trusted and scanned container images. Run containers as non-root where possible, drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, restrict privilege escalation, and use appropriate seccomp and Pod security controls. Apply network policies when supported by the cluster network plugin.\n\nSet realistic resource requests and limits. Configure readiness, liveness, and startup probes according to application behavior. Use controlled rollout strategies, topology-aware scheduling, disruption budgets, and capacity planning for important services.\n\nBack up stateful data and test restoration. Monitor control-plane access, node health, Pod restarts, application SLOs, storage capacity, and cluster resource pressure. Keep cluster versions, add-ons, and workload manifests within supported compatibility ranges.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Code Coverage — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Code Coverage — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply stating that kubectl apply was successful. Explain whether the workload was scheduled, started, became ready, and served real application traffic."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-13-section-2",
        "title": "Code Coverage — Framework Architecture",
        "content": "# Code Coverage — Framework Architecture\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Code Coverage** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nExplain the architecture and responsibilities of the components involved. Describe API requests, authentication and authorization, admission processing, persistence in etcd, controller reconciliation, scheduling, kubelet coordination, and container-runtime interaction where relevant. Identify which component makes the decision, which component executes the work, and how the resulting state becomes visible through the Kubernetes API. Explain the impact of control-plane or node-component failures.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes. The kubelet on each worker node works with the container runtime to ensure assigned workloads run as specified.\n\nFor **Code Coverage — Framework Architecture**, identify the API resource, controller, node component, or external integration responsible for the behavior. Explain what state is submitted, what reconciliation takes place, and how the result can be observed. Do not assume that a successful kubectl apply means the application is healthy. API acceptance, scheduling, container startup, readiness, and application availability are separate conditions.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the current Kubernetes context with kubectl config current-context.\n2. Select and verify the correct namespace.\n3. Inspect the relevant resources before changing anything.\n4. Review the manifest fields, image reference, labels, selectors, resources, and permissions.\n5. Apply the required configuration using a reviewed manifest or approved deployment process.\n6. Observe controller reconciliation and Pod scheduling.\n7. Inspect Pod conditions, container state, events, and logs.\n8. Verify networking, storage, and application readiness where required.\n9. Record the manifest revision, image digest, and resulting workload state.\n\nUse namespaces and explicit resource names to avoid changing the wrong environment. Before deleting or replacing resources, determine whether they own persistent data or production traffic.\n\n## 4. Practical Kubernetes Commands\n\nThe following commands provide relevant inspection and operational examples. Replace placeholder resource names and namespaces with actual values.\n\n`\bash id=\"k8s501\"\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get namespaces\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl get services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n`\n\nCommands that modify resources should be executed only after checking the active context and reviewing the target manifest. Avoid copying production credentials or secret values into terminal history.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes Workload Example\n\nThe following Deployment demonstrates a basic stateless application workload:\n\n`yaml id=\"k8s502\"\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: application\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.0\n          ports:\n            - name: http\n              containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"250m\"\n              memory: \"256Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 30\n            periodSeconds: 10\n`\n\nThe Deployment declares three desired replicas. Its controller creates and maintains the Pods. Resource requests help the scheduler select suitable nodes, while limits constrain container consumption. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic. The example must be adapted to the actual application health endpoints and resource profile.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment application deployed to a Kubernetes cluster. The development team stores its source code and Kubernetes manifests in version control. A CI pipeline builds and tests the application, scans the image, and publishes an immutable image digest to a registry.\n\nA delivery process updates the Deployment manifest to reference the approved image. Kubernetes accepts the updated desired state and begins a rolling update. The scheduler selects nodes with sufficient available resources. Kubelets start the containers, and readiness probes determine when the new Pods can receive traffic.\n\nThe Service routes requests to ready endpoints. Monitoring records application latency, error rates, Pod restarts, CPU usage, memory usage, and node health. If the new version fails readiness or application validation, the delivery process stops or rolls back according to the deployment policy.\n\nThe team records the source commit, image digest, manifest revision, deployment time, and validation result. This makes it possible to identify exactly which application version is running during an incident.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Applying manifests to the wrong Kubernetes context can change an unintended cluster.\n- Incorrect labels or selectors can leave Services without matching Pod endpoints.\n- Missing resource requests can make scheduling unpredictable under resource pressure.\n- Excessively low memory limits can cause containers to be terminated with OOMKilled.\n- Incorrect readiness probes can prevent healthy applications from receiving traffic.\n- Incorrect liveness probes can repeatedly restart an application that is still recovering.\n- Using mutable image tags weakens deployment traceability.\n- Broad RBAC permissions increase the impact of compromised identities.\n- Deleting PersistentVolumeClaims without understanding reclaim behavior can cause data loss.\n- Assuming Pod creation means application readiness can cause outages.\n- Ignoring namespace boundaries can lead to incorrect resource inspection or modification.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by confirming the active context, namespace, resource name, and timestamp of the failure. Inspect resource state using kubectl get and kubectl describe. Review events for scheduling failures, image-pull errors, mount failures, probe failures, and node pressure.\n\nFor a Pending Pod, inspect scheduling events, node capacity, resource requests, taints, tolerations, and affinity. For ImagePullBackOff, verify the image reference, registry availability, and image-pull credentials. For CrashLoopBackOff, inspect current and previous container logs, exit codes, commands, configuration, and resource limits.\n\nFor service connectivity problems, inspect Service selectors, EndpointSlices, DNS, network policies, and the cluster network implementation. For storage failures, inspect PVC binding, PV state, CSI components, access modes, and mount events.\n\nApply the smallest safe correction. Preserve evidence before deleting Pods or changing shared resources. Verify the recovery through resource conditions and an actual application request.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC for human users and service accounts. Avoid granting cluster-admin access to application workloads. Protect Secrets through encryption at rest, restrictive access, controlled rotation, and secure external secret integration where required.\n\nUse trusted and scanned container images. Run containers as non-root where possible, drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, restrict privilege escalation, and use appropriate seccomp and Pod security controls. Apply network policies when supported by the cluster network plugin.\n\nSet realistic resource requests and limits. Configure readiness, liveness, and startup probes according to application behavior. Use controlled rollout strategies, topology-aware scheduling, disruption budgets, and capacity planning for important services.\n\nBack up stateful data and test restoration. Monitor control-plane access, node health, Pod restarts, application SLOs, storage capacity, and cluster resource pressure. Keep cluster versions, add-ons, and workload manifests within supported compatibility ranges.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Code Coverage — Framework Architecture** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Code Coverage — Framework Architecture** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply stating that kubectl apply was successful. Explain whether the workload was scheduled, started, became ready, and served real application traffic."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-13-section-3",
        "title": "Code Coverage — Test Design and Implementation",
        "content": "# Code Coverage — Test Design and Implementation\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Code Coverage** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nDescribe a reproducible Kubernetes exercise. State the cluster prerequisites, namespace, manifests, required images, resource settings, and commands. Explain the expected state after each operation and how to inspect it. Include a controlled failure, such as an invalid image, incorrect Service selector, insufficient resource request, or failing readiness probe. Verify recovery through resource status, events, logs, endpoints, and an application-level request.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes. The kubelet on each worker node works with the container runtime to ensure assigned workloads run as specified.\n\nFor **Code Coverage — Test Design and Implementation**, identify the API resource, controller, node component, or external integration responsible for the behavior. Explain what state is submitted, what reconciliation takes place, and how the result can be observed. Do not assume that a successful kubectl apply means the application is healthy. API acceptance, scheduling, container startup, readiness, and application availability are separate conditions.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the current Kubernetes context with kubectl config current-context.\n2. Select and verify the correct namespace.\n3. Inspect the relevant resources before changing anything.\n4. Review the manifest fields, image reference, labels, selectors, resources, and permissions.\n5. Apply the required configuration using a reviewed manifest or approved deployment process.\n6. Observe controller reconciliation and Pod scheduling.\n7. Inspect Pod conditions, container state, events, and logs.\n8. Verify networking, storage, and application readiness where required.\n9. Record the manifest revision, image digest, and resulting workload state.\n\nUse namespaces and explicit resource names to avoid changing the wrong environment. Before deleting or replacing resources, determine whether they own persistent data or production traffic.\n\n## 4. Practical Kubernetes Commands\n\nThe following commands provide relevant inspection and operational examples. Replace placeholder resource names and namespaces with actual values.\n\n`\bash id=\"k8s501\"\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get namespaces\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl get services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n`\n\nCommands that modify resources should be executed only after checking the active context and reviewing the target manifest. Avoid copying production credentials or secret values into terminal history.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes Workload Example\n\nThe following Deployment demonstrates a basic stateless application workload:\n\n`yaml id=\"k8s502\"\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: application\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.0\n          ports:\n            - name: http\n              containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"250m\"\n              memory: \"256Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 30\n            periodSeconds: 10\n`\n\nThe Deployment declares three desired replicas. Its controller creates and maintains the Pods. Resource requests help the scheduler select suitable nodes, while limits constrain container consumption. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic. The example must be adapted to the actual application health endpoints and resource profile.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment application deployed to a Kubernetes cluster. The development team stores its source code and Kubernetes manifests in version control. A CI pipeline builds and tests the application, scans the image, and publishes an immutable image digest to a registry.\n\nA delivery process updates the Deployment manifest to reference the approved image. Kubernetes accepts the updated desired state and begins a rolling update. The scheduler selects nodes with sufficient available resources. Kubelets start the containers, and readiness probes determine when the new Pods can receive traffic.\n\nThe Service routes requests to ready endpoints. Monitoring records application latency, error rates, Pod restarts, CPU usage, memory usage, and node health. If the new version fails readiness or application validation, the delivery process stops or rolls back according to the deployment policy.\n\nThe team records the source commit, image digest, manifest revision, deployment time, and validation result. This makes it possible to identify exactly which application version is running during an incident.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Applying manifests to the wrong Kubernetes context can change an unintended cluster.\n- Incorrect labels or selectors can leave Services without matching Pod endpoints.\n- Missing resource requests can make scheduling unpredictable under resource pressure.\n- Excessively low memory limits can cause containers to be terminated with OOMKilled.\n- Incorrect readiness probes can prevent healthy applications from receiving traffic.\n- Incorrect liveness probes can repeatedly restart an application that is still recovering.\n- Using mutable image tags weakens deployment traceability.\n- Broad RBAC permissions increase the impact of compromised identities.\n- Deleting PersistentVolumeClaims without understanding reclaim behavior can cause data loss.\n- Assuming Pod creation means application readiness can cause outages.\n- Ignoring namespace boundaries can lead to incorrect resource inspection or modification.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by confirming the active context, namespace, resource name, and timestamp of the failure. Inspect resource state using kubectl get and kubectl describe. Review events for scheduling failures, image-pull errors, mount failures, probe failures, and node pressure.\n\nFor a Pending Pod, inspect scheduling events, node capacity, resource requests, taints, tolerations, and affinity. For ImagePullBackOff, verify the image reference, registry availability, and image-pull credentials. For CrashLoopBackOff, inspect current and previous container logs, exit codes, commands, configuration, and resource limits.\n\nFor service connectivity problems, inspect Service selectors, EndpointSlices, DNS, network policies, and the cluster network implementation. For storage failures, inspect PVC binding, PV state, CSI components, access modes, and mount events.\n\nApply the smallest safe correction. Preserve evidence before deleting Pods or changing shared resources. Verify the recovery through resource conditions and an actual application request.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC for human users and service accounts. Avoid granting cluster-admin access to application workloads. Protect Secrets through encryption at rest, restrictive access, controlled rotation, and secure external secret integration where required.\n\nUse trusted and scanned container images. Run containers as non-root where possible, drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, restrict privilege escalation, and use appropriate seccomp and Pod security controls. Apply network policies when supported by the cluster network plugin.\n\nSet realistic resource requests and limits. Configure readiness, liveness, and startup probes according to application behavior. Use controlled rollout strategies, topology-aware scheduling, disruption budgets, and capacity planning for important services.\n\nBack up stateful data and test restoration. Monitor control-plane access, node health, Pod restarts, application SLOs, storage capacity, and cluster resource pressure. Keep cluster versions, add-ons, and workload manifests within supported compatibility ranges.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Code Coverage — Test Design and Implementation** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Code Coverage — Test Design and Implementation** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected, and the production