        "id": "stage-5-topic-14-section-2",
        "title": "Test Reports — Framework Architecture",
        "content": "# Test Reports — Framework Architecture\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Test Reports** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nExplain the architecture and responsibilities of the components involved. Describe API requests, authentication and authorization, admission processing, persistence in etcd, controller reconciliation, scheduling, kubelet coordination, and container-runtime interaction where relevant. Identify which component makes the decision, which component executes the work, and how the resulting state becomes visible through the Kubernetes API. Explain the impact of control-plane or node-component failures.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes. The kubelet on each worker node works with the container runtime to ensure assigned workloads run as specified.\n\nFor **Test Reports — Framework Architecture**, identify the API resource, controller, node component, or external integration responsible for the behavior. Explain what state is submitted, what reconciliation takes place, and how the result can be observed. Do not assume that a successful kubectl apply means the application is healthy. API acceptance, scheduling, container startup, readiness, and application availability are separate conditions.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the current Kubernetes context with kubectl config current-context.\n2. Select and verify the correct namespace.\n3. Inspect the relevant resources before changing anything.\n4. Review the manifest fields, image reference, labels, selectors, resources, and permissions.\n5. Apply the required configuration using a reviewed manifest or approved deployment process.\n6. Observe controller reconciliation and Pod scheduling.\n7. Inspect Pod conditions, container state, events, and logs.\n8. Verify networking, storage, and application readiness where required.\n9. Record the manifest revision, image digest, and resulting workload state.\n\nUse namespaces and explicit resource names to avoid changing the wrong environment. Before deleting or replacing resources, determine whether they own persistent data or production traffic.\n\n## 4. Practical Kubernetes Commands\n\nThe following commands provide relevant inspection and operational examples. Replace placeholder resource names and namespaces with actual values.\n\n`\bash id=\"k8s501\"\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get namespaces\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl get services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n`\n\nCommands that modify resources should be executed only after checking the active context and reviewing the target manifest. Avoid copying production credentials or secret values into terminal history.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes Workload Example\n\nThe following Deployment demonstrates a basic stateless application workload:\n\n`yaml id=\"k8s502\"\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: application\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.0\n          ports:\n            - name: http\n              containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"250m\"\n              memory: \"256Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 30\n            periodSeconds: 10\n`\n\nThe Deployment declares three desired replicas. Its controller creates and maintains the Pods. Resource requests help the scheduler select suitable nodes, while limits constrain container consumption. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic. The example must be adapted to the actual application health endpoints and resource profile.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment application deployed to a Kubernetes cluster. The development team stores its source code and Kubernetes manifests in version control. A CI pipeline builds and tests the application, scans the image, and publishes an immutable image digest to a registry.\n\nA delivery process updates the Deployment manifest to reference the approved image. Kubernetes accepts the updated desired state and begins a rolling update. The scheduler selects nodes with sufficient available resources. Kubelets start the containers, and readiness probes determine when the new Pods can receive traffic.\n\nThe Service routes requests to ready endpoints. Monitoring records application latency, error rates, Pod restarts, CPU usage, memory usage, and node health. If the new version fails readiness or application validation, the delivery process stops or rolls back according to the deployment policy.\n\nThe team records the source commit, image digest, manifest revision, deployment time, and validation result. This makes it possible to identify exactly which application version is running during an incident.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Applying manifests to the wrong Kubernetes context can change an unintended cluster.\n- Incorrect labels or selectors can leave Services without matching Pod endpoints.\n- Missing resource requests can make scheduling unpredictable under resource pressure.\n- Excessively low memory limits can cause containers to be terminated with OOMKilled.\n- Incorrect readiness probes can prevent healthy applications from receiving traffic.\n- Incorrect liveness probes can repeatedly restart an application that is still recovering.\n- Using mutable image tags weakens deployment traceability.\n- Broad RBAC permissions increase the impact of compromised identities.\n- Deleting PersistentVolumeClaims without understanding reclaim behavior can cause data loss.\n- Assuming Pod creation means application readiness can cause outages.\n- Ignoring namespace boundaries can lead to incorrect resource inspection or modification.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by confirming the active context, namespace, resource name, and timestamp of the failure. Inspect resource state using kubectl get and kubectl describe. Review events for scheduling failures, image-pull errors, mount failures, probe failures, and node pressure.\n\nFor a Pending Pod, inspect scheduling events, node capacity, resource requests, taints, tolerations, and affinity. For ImagePullBackOff, verify the image reference, registry availability, and image-pull credentials. For CrashLoopBackOff, inspect current and previous container logs, exit codes, commands, configuration, and resource limits.\n\nFor service connectivity problems, inspect Service selectors, EndpointSlices, DNS, network policies, and the cluster network implementation. For storage failures, inspect PVC binding, PV state, CSI components, access modes, and mount events.\n\nApply the smallest safe correction. Preserve evidence before deleting Pods or changing shared resources. Verify the recovery through resource conditions and an actual application request.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC for human users and service accounts. Avoid granting cluster-admin access to application workloads. Protect Secrets through encryption at rest, restrictive access, controlled rotation, and secure external secret integration where required.\n\nUse trusted and scanned container images. Run containers as non-root where possible, drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, restrict privilege escalation, and use appropriate seccomp and Pod security controls. Apply network policies when supported by the cluster network plugin.\n\nSet realistic resource requests and limits. Configure readiness, liveness, and startup probes according to application behavior. Use controlled rollout strategies, topology-aware scheduling, disruption budgets, and capacity planning for important services.\n\nBack up stateful data and test restoration. Monitor control-plane access, node health, Pod restarts, application SLOs, storage capacity, and cluster resource pressure. Keep cluster versions, add-ons, and workload manifests within supported compatibility ranges.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Test Reports — Framework Architecture** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Test Reports — Framework Architecture** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply stating that kubectl apply was successful. Explain whether the workload was scheduled, started, became ready, and served real application traffic."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-14-section-3",
        "title": "Test Reports — Test Design and Implementation",
        "content": "# Test Reports — Test Design and Implementation\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Test Reports** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nDescribe a reproducible Kubernetes exercise. State the cluster prerequisites, namespace, manifests, required images, resource settings, and commands. Explain the expected state after each operation and how to inspect it. Include a controlled failure, such as an invalid image, incorrect Service selector, insufficient resource request, or failing readiness probe. Verify recovery through resource status, events, logs, endpoints, and an application-level request.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled